WEBVTT

﻿1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.000
The Odyssey (2026)

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.074
Watch Online Movies and Series for FREE
www.osdb.link/lm

3
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.550
Subtitled by I_Am_Khang

4
00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:35.150
A TIME OF APPARENT MAGIC...

5
00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.250
A face...

6
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:43.670
A fleet...

7
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:47.290
A war...

8
00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:51.790
A man...

9
00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.510
A thought...

10
00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.530
A trick...

11
00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.550
A trick to break the walls of Troy.

12
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:09.530
And burn it!

13
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.170
Scream into the ground!

14
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.480
What does it mean?

15
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:13.750
A gift.

16
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.810
For Athena.

17
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:22.550
Common Gods.

18
00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.550
Respect their offering.

19
00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:30.190
Save it from the waves!

20
00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:31.850
Take it to Athena's temple!

21
00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.130
The war is over!

22
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.500
Stop!

23
00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.600
Not this song.

24
00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:09.120
Mama's boy!

25
00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.120
Run. run. little man!

26
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:13.550
Run!

27
00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.890
I want to remind them of
whose wine they're drinking.

28
00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:18.930
I don't want the Odysseus's song.

29
00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:20.270
I want Odysseus.

30
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.290
Then why do you entertain
suitors in his house night after night?

31
00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:27.470
In this world. a man does what he chooses.

32
00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:29.470
I do what I can.

33
00:03:29.589 --> 00:03:31.510
I am a man now. Mom.

34
00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:33.790
So throw them out.

35
00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.910
Break Zeus's law and see what they
do to you if you give them that excuse.

36
00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.750
I've already lost a husband.

37
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:47.850
I don't want to lose a son.

38
00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:49.570
My dad's coming back.

39
00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:50.970
Where is he?

40
00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.810
Eight years since Troy fell.

41
00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.030
Almost twenty since he left us.

42
00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:57.330
Where is he?

43
00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.150
We have to welcome him
like we welcomed all of you.

44
00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:12.690
Are you calling us beggars?

45
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.030
I'm saying you've been
welcome here for three years.

46
00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.130
We've only enjoyed your
hospitality for so long.

47
00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.310
Because your mother won't choose.

48
00:04:22.129 --> 00:04:23.450
She's already married.

49
00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:24.890
Odysseus is dead.

50
00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:26.890
What do you know about my dad?

51
00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:32.350
I know he kept stringing
cattle and bad wine.

52
00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:33.927
But you had the best day of your life.

53
00:04:33.951 --> 00:04:35.471
The rest of it. the first two seasons.

54
00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.070
I know he never came home.

55
00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.710
Not yet.

56
00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:42.890
You're pining for a daddy
you didn't even know.

57
00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:45.570
Like some sniffling bastard.

58
00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:47.861
Well. since none of us can really know who

59
00:04:47.885 --> 00:04:50.751
our fathers are. don't
claim that I'm lying.

60
00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:51.850
But I did know him.

61
00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:53.270
As boys.

62
00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:54.470
We watched him hunt.

63
00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:58.850
I tried to volunteer for
his expedition to Troy.

64
00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.547
But he told me to stay
here and keep an eye on you.

65
00:05:01.571 --> 00:05:03.110
On me or my mother.

66
00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.610
You can't blame me for looking at me.

67
00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:08.110
My dad is coming home.

68
00:05:08.365 --> 00:05:10.790
And his hospitality won't
be as generous as hers.

69
00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.830
So he doesn't follow Zeus's law then?

70
00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:15.540
Zeus's law tells us to
treat strangers as we be

71
00:05:15.564 --> 00:05:18.351
treated because they
might be gods in disguise.

72
00:05:19.075 --> 00:05:21.550
I think it's established and
tenuous that you are not a god.

73
00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:24.750
Well. this one doesn't smell like a god.

74
00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.840
Wouldn't be much of a disguise if he did.

75
00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.250
Careful!

76
00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:30.990
He might strike you down. right now.

77
00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:37.940
Come.

78
00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.550
That thunder was luck.

79
00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:48.750
I don't know.

80
00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.230
You have wise eyes.

81
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.530
Athena's eyes. Here.

82
00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:54.530
Do you sail with the merchants?

83
00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:56.330
From Sparta.

84
00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.130
Did Menelaus come home from the war?

85
00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:00.130
Years ago.

86
00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:01.930
Odysseus never came back?

87
00:06:02.140 --> 00:06:03.530
We don't even know if he's alive.

88
00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:04.430
Do you know anything?

89
00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:06.230
The song of how he won the war.

90
00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:08.130
We've all heard that. Anything else?

91
00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:09.130
No.

92
00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:14.730
But Sparta is full of Trojan War veterans.

93
00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:16.650
Surely one of them will know something.

94
00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:18.250
Are you Odysseus's son?

95
00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:20.490
Go to Sparta.

96
00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:23.290
Go. And go to Menelaus.

97
00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:24.930
He will welcome you.

98
00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.120
The queen wants to speak to the stranger.

99
00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:32.150
She lives for news.

100
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:36.630
Just don't try to give her
any false hope.

101
00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.690
She has some way of knowing
whether a person is actually seeing Odysseus.

102
00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:41.290
See that man?

103
00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:43.210
He can't see much anymore

104
00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:45.550
but that doesn't stop him 
from beating travelers to death.

105
00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:47.650
in exchange for lies about my father.

106
00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:15.930
No. no.

107
00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.110
Too fast.

108
00:07:17.915 --> 00:07:18.687
How can you know?

109
00:07:18.711 --> 00:07:21.770
You don't need eyes to
hear a hasty combination.

110
00:07:22.675 --> 00:07:23.970
Mentor. push him.

111
00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.150
Too fast.

112
00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.020
He's as friendly as you.

113
00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.660
He only answered to your father.

114
00:07:47.319 --> 00:07:48.520
Where did he get him from?

115
00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:50.580
He met right here.

116
00:07:51.344 --> 00:07:52.344
Ah.

117
00:07:59.295 --> 00:08:01.180
I'll take him.

118
00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:02.600
For your boy?

119
00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:03.660
For a hunting dog.

120
00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:07.200
Damned if he wasn't right.

121
00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:43.450
Hold still.

122
00:08:43.755 --> 00:08:44.910
How bad a scar do you want?

123
00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.250
Why do you always pluck your bow?

124
00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:49.930
It's only fair.

125
00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:51.190
To warn your prey?

126
00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:52.890
To fight with honor.

127
00:08:52.995 --> 00:08:54.555
You weren't fighting. you were hunting.

128
00:08:54.785 --> 00:08:56.910
Ah. hunting's nothing
but fighting with stealth.

129
00:08:57.430 --> 00:08:58.430
Clever.

130
00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:01.020
Clever. but your cleverness
is going to get you in trouble.

131
00:09:01.525 --> 00:09:02.525
Try again.

132
00:09:03.085 --> 00:09:05.350
This time. not as fast or precise.

133
00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:07.770
You can't win with a perfect defense.

134
00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:09.070
Draw the attack.

135
00:09:09.175 --> 00:09:11.175
- How?
- Hesitate.

136
00:09:11.695 --> 00:09:13.950
Open your belly to your opponent's blade.

137
00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:15.970
Their attack becomes your chance.

138
00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:48.660
You teach my father that?

139
00:09:48.995 --> 00:09:50.660
No. no. he taught me.

140
00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.516
He said. "You're not always going to be the

141
00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.121
strongest or the fastest.
so be the smartest."

142
00:09:58.810 --> 00:09:59.810
What would he do. huh?

143
00:10:00.475 --> 00:10:02.875
If he came back here to find
all these suitors in his house?

144
00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:03.320
Don't.

145
00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.180
I can't even let myself
imagine what he'd do to them.

146
00:10:06.465 --> 00:10:07.465
There are so many.

147
00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:11.540
Cut a couple down. the
others cave bullies and cowards.

148
00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:15.980
Most of them were old enough to go to
Troy. but when they saw Agamemnon's sail.

149
00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:20.679
their fathers hid them.

150
00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:28.299
And you knew Agamemnon's war
would take many years and many lives.

151
00:10:33.425 --> 00:10:35.500
So he raised his army by lottery.

152
00:10:38.540 --> 00:10:38.940
Name?

153
00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:39.617
Draw.

154
00:10:39.641 --> 00:10:40.641
Draw.

155
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:40.960
No.

156
00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:42.020
Name?

157
00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:42.700
Antinous.

158
00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:44.060
Draw.

159
00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:46.700
Yes.

160
00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:47.760
By lots?

161
00:10:50.255 --> 00:10:52.240
Antinous told me he volunteered.

162
00:11:02.260 --> 00:11:04.760
Your boy's trying to take Sinon's place.

163
00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:06.540
I'm not going to let him.

164
00:11:06.815 --> 00:11:08.880
He needs to stay here.
take care of his family.

165
00:11:12.009 --> 00:11:13.520
You can watch over your boy too.

166
00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:14.640
Of course.

167
00:11:18.435 --> 00:11:19.435
He's with me.

168
00:11:20.535 --> 00:11:22.500
Well. he wanted Antinous to look after me.

169
00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:23.880
No. no. no.

170
00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:26.400
But Odysseus was
sensitive to a father's shame.

171
00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:28.020
He fell for these fathers.

172
00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:31.920
You and your mother meant
more to him than promises of glory.

173
00:11:33.279 --> 00:11:34.640
So he knew it would be years.

174
00:11:36.005 --> 00:11:37.005
Did he tell my mother?

175
00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:38.980
That's a question for her.

176
00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:42.160
You may ask. is he ever coming back?

177
00:11:43.885 --> 00:11:44.960
I hardly think so.

178
00:11:45.509 --> 00:11:47.160
Never known a dog reach twenty.

179
00:11:48.695 --> 00:11:50.280
But he's long outlived his master.

180
00:12:07.814 --> 00:12:09.550
I didn't mean to stop you.

181
00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:13.630
I'm in no hurry.

182
00:12:15.194 --> 00:12:17.210
You yelled at your
grandfather's burial shroud.

183
00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:18.730
It'll be time to remarry.

184
00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:20.390
Why?

185
00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:27.690
Ithaca's been without a king too long.

186
00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:29.450
They want security.

187
00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:30.610
From what?

188
00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:32.050
The people from the sea.

189
00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.390
That's just traveler's tales.

190
00:12:35.510 --> 00:12:37.310
All we know of the
world is traveler's tales.

191
00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:38.990
That's how we found out Troy had fallen.

192
00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.710
Now they all tell stories of
attacks by people from the sea.

193
00:12:44.030 --> 00:12:45.710
And who are the people from the sea?

194
00:12:46.370 --> 00:12:49.030
No one knows. but the elders
wanted the good to be prepared.

195
00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:49.970
Prepared?

196
00:12:50.485 --> 00:12:52.790
Part of the greatest
civilization ever known.

197
00:12:54.730 --> 00:12:56.007
Look at this great house.

198
00:12:56.031 --> 00:12:58.210
Stones as old as knowledge.

199
00:12:58.945 --> 00:13:01.770
At the mercy of some
greedy. unwanted guests.

200
00:13:02.835 --> 00:13:04.110
The structure's nothing.

201
00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:07.270
Without people's respect for its meaning.

202
00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:08.890
Zeus's law.

203
00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:11.270
To treat others as you would be treated.

204
00:13:11.985 --> 00:13:14.070
And they abuse that and
they destroy everything.

205
00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:17.330
So how could you think
of marrying one of them?

206
00:13:17.930 --> 00:13:22.810
Because a long time ago.
your father asked me to.

207
00:13:23.515 --> 00:13:25.030
Agamemnon is our king.

208
00:13:25.804 --> 00:13:27.590
The Trojans took his brother's wife.

209
00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.230
Or she ran away with them.

210
00:13:29.789 --> 00:13:33.090
Either way. the walls of Troy
are said to be impregnable.

211
00:13:34.495 --> 00:13:38.430
We'll sit outside that great city.
trying to draw them into battle.

212
00:13:40.969 --> 00:13:46.210
As years go by. Telemachus
grows. and you learn life without me.

213
00:13:46.885 --> 00:13:48.750
Menelaus will give up on Helen.

214
00:13:53.895 --> 00:13:58.600
This is Agamemnon's excuse to break
Troy's control of the trading routes.

215
00:14:01.069 --> 00:14:02.280
He won't let it pass.

216
00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:04.340
Ever.

217
00:14:11.119 --> 00:14:12.720
Well. if you refuse…

218
00:14:19.099 --> 00:14:20.099
He'll take our son.

219
00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:23.300
He would never.

220
00:14:24.119 --> 00:14:27.320
He sacrificed his own
daughter for favorable winds.

221
00:14:29.085 --> 00:14:30.880
Wow. the gods want his daughter's life.

222
00:14:31.924 --> 00:14:33.540
The power of a sacrifice.

223
00:14:35.409 --> 00:14:37.780
Is in the cost to the person making it.

224
00:14:41.495 --> 00:14:42.495
That's monstrous.

225
00:14:45.609 --> 00:14:46.609
And committed.

226
00:14:50.065 --> 00:14:51.520
I won't return soon.

227
00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:53.620
If at all.

228
00:14:58.489 --> 00:14:59.489
What if we ran?

229
00:15:01.669 --> 00:15:06.800
Took you past the ship. the brightest
crew. and headed for the horizon.

230
00:15:08.969 --> 00:15:15.040
Held hands on deck as we rode
west at dusk. chasing the escaping sun.

231
00:15:18.739 --> 00:15:20.420
There's a world beyond these walls.

232
00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:26.860
Agamemnon's vengeance
would fall on all of Ithaca.

233
00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:40.860
Promise me if I don't return by
the time Telemachus comes of age.

234
00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:45.900
Promise me you'll take another husband.

235
00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:49.800
Enough sacrifices.

236
00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:51.480
Love's not a sacrifice.

237
00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:52.900
Of course it is.

238
00:15:54.189 --> 00:15:55.556
I don't want you to make it.

239
00:15:55.580 --> 00:15:56.620
It's not up to you.

240
00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:00.400
Promise me.

241
00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:03.400
No.

242
00:16:07.099 --> 00:16:08.480
Promise me you'll come back.

243
00:16:14.399 --> 00:16:15.890
What if I can't?

244
00:16:21.689 --> 00:16:24.110
Then your memory won't
be making my decisions.

245
00:16:29.380 --> 00:16:30.380
Take my pen.

246
00:16:31.375 --> 00:16:32.700
Wear it always.

247
00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:34.500
Athena will bring you home.

248
00:16:34.705 --> 00:16:36.460
I'm not sure it suits me.

249
00:16:39.494 --> 00:16:43.300
When travelers claim to have seen
you. I'll ask where you are wearing.

250
00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:45.020
and I'll know if they're lying.

251
00:16:48.789 --> 00:16:56.121
If the people from the sea are real. right
now. Ithaca has no king or an army.

252
00:16:56.435 --> 00:16:57.840
I could take this role.

253
00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:01.260
Come.

254
00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:14.390
String it.

255
00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:25.400
That's not fair.

256
00:17:26.400 --> 00:17:27.960
You said only he could string this bow.

257
00:17:28.355 --> 00:17:29.780
He was happy to let others try.

258
00:17:32.669 --> 00:17:34.120
He'd line up all the axes.

259
00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:08.360
No one else ever did it.

260
00:18:09.615 --> 00:18:11.899
Mom. they're bleeding us
dry. so you'll be forced to

261
00:18:11.923 --> 00:18:14.281
choose one of them before I
can claim the throne myself.

262
00:18:15.435 --> 00:18:18.220
The traveler told me
Menelaus returned years ago.

263
00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:19.360
They've all returned.

264
00:18:19.520 --> 00:18:20.520
All who survived.

265
00:18:22.490 --> 00:18:23.500
Maybe he is dead.

266
00:18:24.409 --> 00:18:26.220
And if he is. then this is my palace.

267
00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:28.540
We need to know.

268
00:18:28.985 --> 00:18:31.120
I have to go to Menelaus
to find out the truth.

269
00:18:33.220 --> 00:18:35.100
Telemachus. these suitors mean you harm.

270
00:18:35.305 --> 00:18:36.600
Oh. you think I don't know that?

271
00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:40.320
Every night they push me to
give them the excuse they need.

272
00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:43.020
I'm out there on the sea. on the road.

273
00:18:44.120 --> 00:18:45.280
They won't need an excuse.

274
00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:46.400
You just won't come back.

275
00:18:48.709 --> 00:18:50.560
You don't want me to
claim the throne myself.

276
00:18:52.779 --> 00:18:56.500
You just want to keep me in my place
so that you can stay queen of Ithaca.

277
00:18:56.599 --> 00:18:59.420
I've had enough of being
queen of Ithaca without my king.

278
00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:00.770
Without your king?

279
00:19:02.559 --> 00:19:03.559
Or a king?

280
00:19:04.919 --> 00:19:07.579
And you think you're ready
to take your father's place?

281
00:19:09.860 --> 00:19:10.860
Your child.

282
00:19:21.400 --> 00:19:23.140
Too loud. Melanthias.

283
00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:25.340
Don't wear your spurs in the palace.

284
00:19:25.600 --> 00:19:27.497
You're cowherd. not a horseman.

285
00:19:27.521 --> 00:19:30.700
And you're a pig farmer. and
who are you to talk to me like that?

286
00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:34.780
A servant our master left
in charge of his herdsman.

287
00:19:35.130 --> 00:19:38.260
The master is long
gone. you blind old fool.

288
00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:41.920
And you're about as
welcome here as that dog ass.

289
00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:43.860
Argus!

290
00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:48.280
Argus!

291
00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:50.700
Argus!

292
00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:52.080
Argus!

293
00:19:53.509 --> 00:19:55.717
Sir. he didn't do anything.

294
00:19:55.741 --> 00:19:56.760
So that stink is you?

295
00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.320
Leave the old dog alone.

296
00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:00.480
Which one?

297
00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:01.720
Both of us.

298
00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:03.956
What did you say to me. slave?

299
00:20:03.980 --> 00:20:04.996
You just got carried away.

300
00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:06.700
Argus is my father's hunting dog.

301
00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:07.960
His hunting dog?

302
00:20:11.920 --> 00:20:13.000
Look at that thing.

303
00:20:14.860 --> 00:20:15.860
Get it out of here.

304
00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:16.360
Don't!

305
00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:17.600
Can't you see he's in pain?

306
00:20:18.300 --> 00:20:19.300
Okay.

307
00:20:19.700 --> 00:20:21.100
Then I'll put it out of his misery.

308
00:20:21.140 --> 00:20:22.140
Leave him alone!

309
00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:29.180
Come on.

310
00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:40.660
Do you see?

311
00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:42.900
You all see?

312
00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:45.920
Here. touch me!

313
00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:48.466
I have the right.

314
00:20:51.831 --> 00:20:53.250
Please don't hurt my son.

315
00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:57.780
Easy. easy.

316
00:20:58.989 --> 00:21:00.250
Young pup lost his head.

317
00:21:00.470 --> 00:21:01.470
Show some mercy.

318
00:21:03.910 --> 00:21:05.030
Drink some more of his wine.

319
00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:06.190
I'll take care of this.

320
00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:08.690
It's my father's hunting dog.

321
00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:12.250
He's only living to see Odysseus return.

322
00:21:12.450 --> 00:21:13.010
He's fine.

323
00:21:13.270 --> 00:21:16.690
I'll put him on a dung heap out front so
he can greet his master a little sooner.

324
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:28.700
That dog stays out there!

325
00:21:30.620 --> 00:21:33.260
Next time think twice before
you offer up your life for an animal.

326
00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:46.290
You're clear.

327
00:21:48.290 --> 00:21:48.930
Prepare supplies.

328
00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:49.870
Take them down to the ships.

329
00:21:50.070 --> 00:21:51.070
Tell no one.

330
00:21:51.250 --> 00:21:52.450
Use only people you trust.

331
00:21:52.850 --> 00:21:54.186
You've freaked your mother's heart.

332
00:21:54.210 --> 00:21:56.090
If I stay. they'll force me to fight.

333
00:21:56.630 --> 00:21:57.630
No one will back me.

334
00:21:57.970 --> 00:21:59.810
I have to find out what
happened to my father.

335
00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:03.330
Ithaca will be behind me if
they know Odysseus is dead.

336
00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:04.950
The suitors will never allow that.

337
00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:06.407
You will never make it back alive.

338
00:22:06.431 --> 00:22:09.590
Tell Mentor to meet me at
the ships just before dawn.

339
00:22:10.190 --> 00:22:12.930
What do you want me to tell your mother?

340
00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:15.710
To hold out for as long as you can.

341
00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:19.980
Ship's ready.

342
00:22:20.480 --> 00:22:21.480
Tide's right.

343
00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:24.060
Have I ever told you
that you have wise eyes?

344
00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:25.720
Athena's eyes?

345
00:22:26.100 --> 00:22:27.100
Once or twice.

346
00:22:27.380 --> 00:22:29.300
You haven't even asked
me where we're going yet.

347
00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:30.420
Where are we going?

348
00:22:30.960 --> 00:22:31.960
We'll find my dad.

349
00:22:32.660 --> 00:22:33.300
He's alive.

350
00:22:33.420 --> 00:22:35.040
I can feel it in the wind and the waves.

351
00:22:35.200 --> 00:22:36.200
He's out there somewhere.

352
00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:38.240
Wandering. injured. lost.

353
00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:39.300
I don't know.

354
00:22:39.835 --> 00:22:40.835
But he's alive.

355
00:23:12.809 --> 00:23:13.809
More dreams?

356
00:23:16.374 --> 00:23:18.821
Men drowning… inside.

357
00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:22.981
Some… dark space.

358
00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:27.020
Firelight on stone.

359
00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:28.400
I don't know.

360
00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:32.000
Don't you want to remember?

361
00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:34.860
Why can't God speak in ways we understand?

362
00:23:37.529 --> 00:23:39.340
Who doesn't understand thunder?

363
00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:41.280
The fire.

364
00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:43.340
A child's smile.

365
00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:44.720
Good harvest.

366
00:23:45.320 --> 00:23:46.800
You hide yourselves from us.

367
00:23:48.920 --> 00:23:49.920
Look.

368
00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:51.800
Who's moving that cloud?

369
00:23:53.220 --> 00:23:54.220
Massing those birds?

370
00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:56.960
Who's moving the water?

371
00:23:59.380 --> 00:24:00.560
Who were you talking to?

372
00:24:03.580 --> 00:24:05.360
Athena came to me again.

373
00:24:05.870 --> 00:24:07.120
She's telling me to remember.

374
00:24:09.314 --> 00:24:10.314
To leave.

375
00:24:11.000 --> 00:24:12.000
Why would you leave?

376
00:24:12.204 --> 00:24:13.460
We have everything we need.

377
00:24:14.580 --> 00:24:16.400
How long have I been here. Calypso?

378
00:24:20.860 --> 00:24:22.680
I have a home somewhere.

379
00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:27.841
There's someone… out there.

380
00:24:28.350 --> 00:24:29.350
How do you know?

381
00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:31.060
I don't know.

382
00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:34.400
I feel.

383
00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:52.380
What we have here could last forever.

384
00:24:52.940 --> 00:24:53.940
Forever is for God.

385
00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:55.740
For your God.

386
00:24:58.220 --> 00:25:01.720
You want to remember. but what if
remembering destroys your happiness?

387
00:25:02.525 --> 00:25:03.525
Then it wasn't real.

388
00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:12.710
No.

389
00:25:12.950 --> 00:25:13.550
No more.

390
00:25:13.650 --> 00:25:14.650
But I love it.

391
00:25:15.190 --> 00:25:16.410
Stop eating the lotus.

392
00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:18.590
Tell me what you remember.

393
00:25:20.564 --> 00:25:21.590
After Troy.

394
00:25:23.830 --> 00:25:24.830
Troy.

395
00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:31.960
We won the war.

396
00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:34.060
And then what?

397
00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:41.650
Ten years on this fucking beach.

398
00:26:46.790 --> 00:26:47.930
Let's go home.

399
00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:51.030
Remember who we leave here.

400
00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:57.260
Do we have enough provisions?

401
00:26:58.420 --> 00:26:59.700
We've taken enough from Troy.

402
00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:02.560
Another stop or two won't kill us.

403
00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:08.960
And pull!

404
00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:12.160
And pull!

405
00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:15.861
And… Stow the oars.

406
00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:17.360
Rest!

407
00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:19.560
Stow the oars!

408
00:27:25.820 --> 00:27:27.120
We'll raise the sail.

409
00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:28.500
Follow the southerly winds.

410
00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:29.820
Yes. sir.

411
00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:32.960
Agamemnon's rowing west
on the known provisions route.

412
00:27:33.240 --> 00:27:34.400
He's got more mouths to feed.

413
00:27:35.500 --> 00:27:36.617
We'll find places to land.

414
00:27:36.641 --> 00:27:38.080
Are you sure it's a risk?

415
00:27:38.480 --> 00:27:40.320
We've followed Agamemnon long enough.

416
00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:43.959
With a little luck and
the right winds. we'll see

417
00:27:43.983 --> 00:27:46.700
some of the world and still
be home before Agamemnon.

418
00:28:56.450 --> 00:28:58.260
Just keep the bear on the right.

419
00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:09.240
Odysseus!

420
00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:10.540
Man!

421
00:29:13.410 --> 00:29:14.410
See?

422
00:29:15.070 --> 00:29:16.390
You lucky bastard.

423
00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:18.770
We're down to our last supplies.

424
00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:20.490
Assemble a landing party.

425
00:29:20.890 --> 00:29:21.550
Food and water.

426
00:29:21.690 --> 00:29:22.690
Enough for three weeks.

427
00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:24.670
What if they won't give it?

428
00:29:26.150 --> 00:29:27.870
How badly do you want to get home?

429
00:29:29.150 --> 00:29:30.590
Get your weapons ready!

430
00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:32.150
Begging.

431
00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:33.550
Borrowing.

432
00:29:34.250 --> 00:29:35.250
Pondering.

433
00:29:38.730 --> 00:29:42.167
Ask him why they abandoned
their village instead of welcoming us.

434
00:29:42.191 --> 00:29:44.350
They see them as Kalasori sit down.

435
00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:46.130
Exeron!

436
00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:47.966
They're waiting for the sea people.

437
00:29:47.990 --> 00:29:48.990
They're waiting for us.

438
00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:50.690
They thought we were the sea people?

439
00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:51.890
No.

440
00:29:52.030 --> 00:29:53.030
We're Greeks.

441
00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:54.850
From Ithaca.

442
00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:56.850
Maybe you heard our victory at Troy.

443
00:29:57.790 --> 00:29:59.850
We're headed west on the southerly winds.

444
00:30:00.770 --> 00:30:01.227
We're headed west on the southerly winds.

445
00:30:01.251 --> 00:30:02.207
We're headed west on the southerly winds.

446
00:30:02.231 --> 00:30:10.070
He says if we go that way. we
won't like the things we'll find.

447
00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:17.970
After years of war. no one could
stand between my men and home.

448
00:30:19.330 --> 00:30:20.330
Not even me.

449
00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:23.150
Didn't something happen?

450
00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:41.200
Chasing the escaping sun?

451
00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:47.440
There you go.

452
00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:51.620
Lucky bastard.

453
00:31:03.975 --> 00:31:06.200
No. no. no. no.

454
00:31:06.359 --> 00:31:07.960
Sheep come in flocks.

455
00:31:09.885 --> 00:31:10.885
Let's follow.

456
00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:32.490
Someone lives here.

457
00:32:34.210 --> 00:32:35.210
Yeah. they make cheese.

458
00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:55.010
Pretty good.

459
00:32:58.619 --> 00:32:59.619
There's a lot of it.

460
00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:00.780
We should leave.

461
00:33:01.489 --> 00:33:02.910
We brought nothing to trade with.

462
00:33:03.715 --> 00:33:04.715
Zeus's law.

463
00:33:05.024 --> 00:33:07.286
I don't know if whoever lives
here has heard of Zeus's law.

464
00:33:07.310 --> 00:33:08.730
Or Zeus. for that matter.

465
00:33:08.845 --> 00:33:10.310
Everyone's heard of Zeus's law.

466
00:33:11.104 --> 00:33:12.110
Get the fire going.

467
00:33:12.430 --> 00:33:13.750
We'll welcome the owner back.

468
00:34:33.439 --> 00:34:34.439
Get away from the fire.

469
00:34:54.510 --> 00:35:02.510
I think it's asleep.

470
00:37:00.350 --> 00:37:01.350
Wait.

471
00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:06.651
The… Rock.

472
00:37:13.700 --> 00:37:14.700
We can't move the rock.

473
00:37:14.989 --> 00:37:15.989
We'd be trapped.

474
00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:25.570
Spread the word.

475
00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:27.050
In the morning.

476
00:37:27.190 --> 00:37:30.050
When it lets out its flock.
we'll rush the entrance.

477
00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:31.950
Pressed up.

478
00:37:56.760 --> 00:37:57.760
Go!

479
00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:09.920
Go!

480
00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:14.640
Can

481
00:38:17.700 --> 00:38:20.780
we jam something in the cave mouth?

482
00:38:21.440 --> 00:38:22.440
Leave a gap?

483
00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:24.240
Maybe.

484
00:38:25.000 --> 00:38:26.000
But what?

485
00:38:26.800 --> 00:38:27.800
His law.

486
00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:32.200
It'll notice.

487
00:38:34.419 --> 00:38:35.480
But grab the thin end.

488
00:38:35.804 --> 00:38:36.804
Put it in the fire.

489
00:38:37.840 --> 00:38:39.100
How much rope do we have?

490
00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:40.960
How big is the flock?

491
00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:42.360
Couple dozen. maybe.

492
00:38:45.020 --> 00:38:46.020
Not too many of us.

493
00:38:46.770 --> 00:38:49.320
When it comes back.
two of us have to get out.

494
00:38:49.370 --> 00:38:50.500
Or at least get killed.

495
00:38:55.840 --> 00:38:59.060
Run low through the flock towards
the entrance and then split wide.

496
00:38:59.159 --> 00:39:00.760
So we can only grab one of them.

497
00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:01.340
Right.

498
00:39:01.739 --> 00:39:03.120
Four of us should make the run.

499
00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:04.260
Two and two.

500
00:39:04.549 --> 00:39:05.920
Why risk more men?

501
00:39:06.164 --> 00:39:07.484
There ain't two of us last night.

502
00:39:07.760 --> 00:39:09.440
We're warming up the two before he sleeps.

503
00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:35.870
No!

504
00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:38.307
No!

505
00:40:38.331 --> 00:40:39.331
No!

506
00:40:46.970 --> 00:40:47.970
No!

507
00:40:50.750 --> 00:40:51.750
No!

508
00:40:56.890 --> 00:40:58.290
NOOOOOO!

509
00:40:59.310 --> 00:41:00.267
NOOOO!

510
00:41:00.291 --> 00:41:01.291
No!

511
00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:33.610
No!

512
00:41:34.390 --> 00:41:34.390
No!

513
00:41:34.430 --> 00:41:34.430
No!

514
00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:34.446
No!

515
00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:34.447
No!

516
00:41:34.471 --> 00:41:35.471
No!

517
00:41:35.609 --> 00:41:36.609
No!

518
00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:42.070
The sun rises for me.

519
00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:43.610
It can talk.

520
00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:46.350
Why not talk before?

521
00:41:48.330 --> 00:41:49.390
Do you talk to ants?

522
00:41:50.824 --> 00:41:52.690
Should we reason with it?

523
00:41:55.410 --> 00:41:56.670
I think we're past that.

524
00:42:53.640 --> 00:43:01.640
What are you doing?

525
00:43:10.260 --> 00:43:11.260
No!

526
00:43:59.980 --> 00:44:01.380
Go!

527
00:44:03.260 --> 00:44:04.660
Go!

528
00:44:26.320 --> 00:44:28.460
Go. go. go. go. go!

529
00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:35.020
Why

530
00:44:39.480 --> 00:44:43.460
do you

531
00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:53.930
have to fire that arrow?

532
00:44:54.550 --> 00:44:56.290
We lost good soldiers to that beast.

533
00:44:56.830 --> 00:44:59.310
Yeah. and that beast
was the son of Poseidon.

534
00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:00.230
What?

535
00:45:01.010 --> 00:45:03.230
When we put its eye out.
we heard it crying his name.

536
00:45:03.750 --> 00:45:04.850
That thing was a monster.

537
00:45:05.370 --> 00:45:06.370
I heard it too.

538
00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:08.870
So now the sea is against us?

539
00:45:09.190 --> 00:45:10.007
And the wind.

540
00:45:10.031 --> 00:45:13.750
Why are we supposed to get
home with Poseidon against us?

541
00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:16.110
We can't live by omens and sacrifices.

542
00:45:18.534 --> 00:45:20.347
Do you know how many times I've heard you

543
00:45:20.371 --> 00:45:22.530
weeping. praying to
Athena since we left Troy?

544
00:45:23.124 --> 00:45:24.456
Couldn't you have shown some mercy?

545
00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:26.970
Take this.

546
00:45:29.230 --> 00:45:30.230
Wear it.

547
00:45:30.964 --> 00:45:32.590
Remember those we couldn't bury.

548
00:45:34.330 --> 00:45:36.410
The gods helped those
who helped themselves.

549
00:45:40.370 --> 00:45:45.470
From that moment. every rough sea or
headwind told the crew we were cursed.

550
00:45:47.890 --> 00:45:49.230
But where is my crew?

551
00:45:51.630 --> 00:45:53.050
When will you let me see my men?

552
00:45:54.130 --> 00:45:55.866
If I could just talk to
them. they'd help me.

553
00:45:55.890 --> 00:45:56.890
Let me remember.

554
00:45:57.170 --> 00:45:59.390
Did I have a wife. children. maybe a son?

555
00:45:59.990 --> 00:46:01.210
How long have I been here?

556
00:46:02.930 --> 00:46:03.930
A long time.

557
00:46:04.830 --> 00:46:09.550
If I had a son. how old would he be now?

558
00:46:11.950 --> 00:46:13.450
I don't know how to speak to a king.

559
00:46:14.090 --> 00:46:15.090
You'll be fine.

560
00:46:15.470 --> 00:46:16.287
Keep your mouth shut.

561
00:46:16.311 --> 00:46:19.970
But if anyone asks.
we're traders from Crete.

562
00:46:19.994 --> 00:46:23.011
and we'll figure out
how to approach the king.

563
00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:34.097
Welcome.

564
00:46:34.121 --> 00:46:35.121
Thank you.

565
00:46:35.620 --> 00:46:37.220
You seem to be having quite… This way.

566
00:46:42.620 --> 00:46:43.760
Where are you taking us?

567
00:46:44.100 --> 00:46:46.480
The king insists on
meeting strangers himself.

568
00:47:11.950 --> 00:47:12.527
Sit.

569
00:47:12.551 --> 00:47:13.551
Not you.

570
00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:16.630
You.

571
00:47:18.450 --> 00:47:21.550
Enjoy our hospitality. as Zeus demands.

572
00:47:22.710 --> 00:47:24.430
But perhaps you should
tell us who you are.

573
00:47:25.730 --> 00:47:27.170
We picked the right day to arrive.

574
00:47:27.670 --> 00:47:28.910
Feast of my daughter's wedding.

575
00:47:29.670 --> 00:47:31.870
Tomorrow she leaves to start a new life.

576
00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:34.410
You don't look like a beggar.

577
00:47:35.330 --> 00:47:36.890
Imagine you know who I am.

578
00:47:38.530 --> 00:47:45.370
I now ask the son of Atreus. brother
of Agamemnon. the husband of Helen.

579
00:47:46.550 --> 00:47:47.950
Perhaps you've heard of Helen.

580
00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:51.330
Face.

581
00:47:52.030 --> 00:47:53.630
I launched a thousand ships.

582
00:47:55.510 --> 00:47:57.470
That may be now just five hundred.

583
00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:05.440
Somebody left on the
battlefields of Troy for that face.

584
00:48:05.860 --> 00:48:07.580
For your brother's ambition.

585
00:48:08.309 --> 00:48:10.260
This one looks familiar.

586
00:48:11.460 --> 00:48:12.480
Hey. Jax.

587
00:48:14.140 --> 00:48:15.140
Chocolates.

588
00:48:15.420 --> 00:48:16.420
Killies.

589
00:48:16.679 --> 00:48:19.720
I don't miss them as much
as one particular warrior.

590
00:48:20.939 --> 00:48:22.480
He was the smartest of us all.

591
00:48:25.220 --> 00:48:27.000
Ten years we rotted on that beach.

592
00:48:40.890 --> 00:48:41.890
Deadlocked.

593
00:48:42.610 --> 00:48:44.330
Fighting amongst ourselves until one day.

594
00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:49.130
They found a way.

595
00:48:50.990 --> 00:48:51.990
Odysseus.

596
00:48:52.890 --> 00:48:54.290
Did we hear the story of the horse?

597
00:48:58.110 --> 00:48:59.470
What did you hear from the inside?

598
00:49:03.859 --> 00:49:05.270
We were in there for days.

599
00:49:06.704 --> 00:49:09.170
We squeezed inside
before the ship set sail.

600
00:49:10.890 --> 00:49:13.330
Odysseus. he knew that no one
could know that we were inside.

601
00:49:13.410 --> 00:49:15.630
Not even sending the
soldier we left behind.

602
00:49:20.990 --> 00:49:24.850
The first two tides our men
drowned right in front of us.

603
00:49:29.630 --> 00:49:33.430
We were silent. between piss and shit.

604
00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:03.860
A gift for Athena.

605
00:50:06.240 --> 00:50:07.500
Common gods.

606
00:50:08.720 --> 00:50:09.720
Respect their offering.

607
00:51:01.600 --> 00:51:09.600
A gift for Athena.

608
00:51:25.310 --> 00:51:27.730
A gift for Athena.

609
00:51:57.150 --> 00:51:58.830
It's enough.

610
00:52:07.520 --> 00:52:10.180
It's enough for Athena.

611
00:54:49.580 --> 00:54:53.520
He knew to wait for a moonless
night around the plains of Troy.

612
00:56:38.220 --> 00:56:39.620
Come

613
00:56:44.920 --> 00:56:46.320
on!

614
00:56:46.460 --> 00:56:47.620
Come on!

615
00:56:48.520 --> 00:56:50.520
Come on!

616
00:57:20.140 --> 00:57:21.780
I think you know the rest.

617
00:57:22.154 --> 00:57:23.700
You're moved by my telling it.

618
00:57:23.794 --> 00:57:25.114
You sure you're the bard singing?

619
00:57:25.689 --> 00:57:26.689
I have.

620
00:57:29.875 --> 00:57:31.820
But they weren't there with all of you.

621
00:57:31.974 --> 00:57:34.380
With your father. Odysseus.

622
00:57:37.365 --> 00:57:38.720
You still like him?

623
00:57:39.754 --> 00:57:41.320
When did you last see him?

624
00:57:42.120 --> 00:57:43.120
Troy.

625
00:57:43.720 --> 00:57:45.560
The gods granted us a righteous victory.

626
00:57:45.880 --> 00:57:46.880
Righteous.

627
00:57:47.460 --> 00:57:51.380
The gods granted our righteous
victory but did not bless our voyages.

628
00:57:52.760 --> 00:57:54.340
Took me years to get home.

629
00:57:55.144 --> 00:57:55.780
But he didn't.

630
00:57:56.084 --> 00:57:57.820
Some did and wish they hadn't.

631
00:57:58.640 --> 00:57:59.640
Tell him.

632
00:58:00.120 --> 00:58:01.680
Tell him about your brother.

633
00:58:02.160 --> 00:58:05.560
About the fate the gods bestowed
on him after your righteous struggle.

634
00:58:06.240 --> 00:58:07.420
About his hero's welcome.

635
00:58:07.620 --> 00:58:10.860
About how Agamemnon
made it home years before us.

636
00:58:11.080 --> 00:58:14.757
Tell him how he was welcomed by
his queen. my twin sister Clytemnestra.

637
00:58:14.781 --> 00:58:15.220
No.

638
00:58:15.520 --> 00:58:15.660
No.

639
00:58:16.020 --> 00:58:16.117
No.

640
00:58:16.141 --> 00:58:17.141
Tell him how she… No!

641
00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:19.260
He does not want to hear about my brother!

642
00:58:22.344 --> 00:58:23.520
He must know of his father.

643
00:58:24.554 --> 00:58:27.140
I know nothing of Odysseus since Troy.

644
00:58:27.240 --> 00:58:28.240
Nothing?

645
00:58:29.360 --> 00:58:31.080
Not from any of your men?

646
00:58:32.320 --> 00:58:34.617
Or Agamemnon's merchants
that travel the rest of the world?

647
00:58:34.641 --> 00:58:35.580
Did you come here for rumors?

648
00:58:35.640 --> 00:58:36.956
Is that what you're interested in?

649
00:58:36.980 --> 00:58:37.980
Gossip?

650
00:58:38.224 --> 00:58:39.340
Are you in the right place?

651
00:58:39.980 --> 00:58:42.320
Anyone want to spin a
story about Odysseus?

652
00:58:42.870 --> 00:58:45.520
Some say he's rich or some say poor.

653
00:58:46.775 --> 00:58:49.540
Some say he perished.
some say he's imprisoned.

654
00:58:49.720 --> 00:58:50.720
What say you?

655
00:58:50.820 --> 00:58:51.820
Imprisoned?

656
00:58:53.960 --> 00:58:57.840
What kind of prison
could hold a man like that?

657
00:58:59.449 --> 00:59:00.920
Tell me what you do. remember.

658
00:59:01.760 --> 00:59:02.760
The rest will come.

659
00:59:04.120 --> 00:59:07.420
And the men became more and more
convinced that I defended Poseidon.

660
00:59:29.110 --> 00:59:30.110
Hey!

661
00:59:31.730 --> 00:59:32.730
We.

662
00:59:53.250 --> 00:59:57.270
Were hit by storm after
storm. all pushing us off course.

663
00:59:57.630 --> 00:59:59.370
Until we were truly lost.

664
01:00:18.130 --> 01:00:19.130
Hello.

665
01:00:55.245 --> 01:00:56.245
We're here in peace.

666
01:00:59.310 --> 01:01:00.440
We just need some food.

667
01:01:06.660 --> 01:01:07.660
We need food.

668
01:01:09.625 --> 01:01:10.720
Can't you understand that?

669
01:01:10.760 --> 01:01:11.760
It's just a child.

670
01:01:14.740 --> 01:01:16.420
If that's a child. what about its parents?

671
01:01:19.089 --> 01:01:20.720
Hey. shh. it's okay.

672
01:01:21.760 --> 01:01:22.537
No. no. no. no.

673
01:01:22.561 --> 01:01:24.020
Shh!

674
01:01:26.340 --> 01:01:27.500
Go!

675
01:01:44.060 --> 01:01:44.460
Go!

676
01:01:45.040 --> 01:01:46.260
Back to the ship!

677
01:01:47.020 --> 01:01:55.020
Go. go. go!

678
01:03:58.370 --> 01:03:59.770
Go!

679
01:04:02.810 --> 01:04:04.210
Go!

680
01:04:09.150 --> 01:04:09.827
Wait!

681
01:04:09.851 --> 01:04:10.851
Checkmate!

682
01:04:12.830 --> 01:04:13.830
Wait!

683
01:04:32.180 --> 01:04:33.960
Move this bastard!

684
01:05:27.960 --> 01:05:30.480
Turn to port. follow the coast.

685
01:05:31.540 --> 01:05:32.640
What if they have ships?

686
01:05:32.840 --> 01:05:34.820
We need to get as far away as possible.

687
01:05:39.150 --> 01:05:41.530
We don't have supplies
for a blind crossing.

688
01:05:42.130 --> 01:05:43.510
We've lost our way.

689
01:05:44.110 --> 01:05:47.130
We need to find food and get our bearings.

690
01:05:50.160 --> 01:05:51.160
Turn to port.

691
01:06:17.930 --> 01:06:18.930
There!

692
01:06:19.070 --> 01:06:20.070
Smoke there!

693
01:06:21.030 --> 01:06:23.470
Go fast. we'll find the
anchorage at the beach.

694
01:06:31.140 --> 01:06:32.140
Who is that?

695
01:06:35.970 --> 01:06:37.570
Former landing party.

696
01:06:40.170 --> 01:06:41.170
Tomorrow.

697
01:06:43.630 --> 01:06:44.630
Tomorrow.

698
01:06:55.300 --> 01:06:56.680
Leave someone with the tenders.

699
01:06:57.894 --> 01:06:59.320
We think you should stay.

700
01:07:01.740 --> 01:07:02.300
We?

701
01:07:02.740 --> 01:07:03.740
All of us.

702
01:07:04.475 --> 01:07:05.475
Still alive.

703
01:07:16.350 --> 01:07:17.350
Is this a mutiny?

704
01:07:17.940 --> 01:07:18.940
That's up to you.

705
01:07:23.415 --> 01:07:24.415
Give me your boat.

706
01:07:24.775 --> 01:07:25.975
I'll make sure we eat tonight.

707
01:07:26.860 --> 01:07:27.880
No matter what you find.

708
01:09:14.910 --> 01:09:16.210
No. no. it's okay!

709
01:09:16.550 --> 01:09:17.550
It's okay!

710
01:09:22.010 --> 01:09:23.010
You're okay!

711
01:09:23.130 --> 01:09:24.010
It's all right!

712
01:09:24.090 --> 01:09:25.090
No one's gonna hurt you!

713
01:09:25.170 --> 01:09:26.470
If I do what you want.

714
01:09:26.595 --> 01:09:28.730
No one is going to hurt you.

715
01:09:32.420 --> 01:09:33.697
My name's Eurylochus.

716
01:09:33.721 --> 01:09:34.721
What's your name?

717
01:09:34.960 --> 01:09:35.340
Cersei.

718
01:09:35.940 --> 01:09:36.940
Cersei?

719
01:09:37.020 --> 01:09:37.860
We need food.

720
01:09:37.944 --> 01:09:38.944
Badly.

721
01:09:39.880 --> 01:09:40.880
I have food.

722
01:09:42.800 --> 01:09:43.880
Lots of food.

723
01:10:05.110 --> 01:10:06.359
You let me?

724
01:10:09.600 --> 01:10:11.279
Your weapons were armor.

725
01:10:13.245 --> 01:10:14.245
Put it out the back.

726
01:10:50.620 --> 01:10:51.720
Put it in.

727
01:10:56.859 --> 01:10:58.520
You like my meat?

728
01:10:59.620 --> 01:11:01.779
Why do you have so much?

729
01:11:02.740 --> 01:11:05.120
You never know when strangers will come.

730
01:11:15.830 --> 01:11:16.530
Men

731
01:11:18.899 --> 01:11:20.689
with their appetites.

732
01:11:22.670 --> 01:11:24.210
Too much is never enough.

733
01:11:33.930 --> 01:11:35.330
No!

734
01:12:17.020 --> 01:12:18.420
No!

735
01:12:41.580 --> 01:12:42.580
Eurylochus!

736
01:12:45.400 --> 01:12:46.400
Eurylochus!

737
01:13:07.600 --> 01:13:08.880
They haven't come back yet!

738
01:13:09.000 --> 01:13:09.480
None of them?

739
01:13:09.640 --> 01:13:09.840
No!

740
01:13:10.340 --> 01:13:11.340
Give me your dagger.

741
01:13:14.590 --> 01:13:15.710
Stay with the boat!

742
01:13:56.795 --> 01:13:58.450
Why don't you just come here?

743
01:14:06.780 --> 01:14:08.610
Do you come from the sea?

744
01:14:10.500 --> 01:14:11.530
Are you alone?

745
01:14:16.850 --> 01:14:19.730
My pigs have taken a liking to you.

746
01:14:20.420 --> 01:14:21.420
Are you hungry?

747
01:14:23.830 --> 01:14:27.750
Leave your weapons at the door
and enjoy the hospitality of Zeus.

748
01:14:29.490 --> 01:14:30.550
Grab a hog.

749
01:14:30.710 --> 01:14:32.190
You can slaughter it for your dinner.

750
01:14:57.300 --> 01:14:59.820
Whoever you are. you're
clearly not a farmer.

751
01:15:00.700 --> 01:15:01.700
Here.

752
01:15:04.920 --> 01:15:06.070
That stew smells good.

753
01:15:10.140 --> 01:15:11.330
That's enough for just us.

754
01:15:12.790 --> 01:15:14.410
Who says it's just us?

755
01:15:16.450 --> 01:15:17.450
Who else is here?

756
01:15:18.070 --> 01:15:19.070
Who indeed?

757
01:15:32.320 --> 01:15:36.070
You say you're all alone.
but no one comes here alone.

758
01:15:37.250 --> 01:15:38.250
No?

759
01:15:38.560 --> 01:15:40.810
You can only reach this spot by sea.

760
01:15:41.790 --> 01:15:43.550
So. where are your shipmates?

761
01:15:44.790 --> 01:15:46.130
I'm looking for them.

762
01:15:47.290 --> 01:15:48.670
Anyone happen to come this way?

763
01:15:51.710 --> 01:15:53.370
But you prepared all this food.

764
01:15:53.680 --> 01:15:54.770
You're questioning me?

765
01:15:55.310 --> 01:15:56.310
You?

766
01:15:58.020 --> 01:16:02.430
The traveler who's clearly starving. he
hasn't touched the food in front of him.

767
01:16:02.835 --> 01:16:05.870
Claims to be a sailor. yet
wears a great warrior's armor.

768
01:16:07.360 --> 01:16:12.110
And who breaks Zeus's law by bringing
a dagger into his hostess's house.

769
01:16:12.675 --> 01:16:13.690
You have sharp eyes.

770
01:16:14.465 --> 01:16:15.710
Sharpen by the gods.

771
01:16:16.720 --> 01:16:17.890
Do they grant you insights?

772
01:16:19.790 --> 01:16:20.790
Truths.

773
01:16:21.350 --> 01:16:22.350
Sometimes.

774
01:16:22.755 --> 01:16:24.670
Would they tell you
where I could find my men?

775
01:16:25.675 --> 01:16:26.675
You're a man.

776
01:16:27.010 --> 01:16:28.010
That's right.

777
01:16:28.630 --> 01:16:29.630
They would.

778
01:16:29.815 --> 01:16:30.815
Then would you tell me?

779
01:16:31.785 --> 01:16:32.785
Perhaps.

780
01:16:34.530 --> 01:16:35.950
If you give me your dagger.

781
01:16:44.530 --> 01:16:47.866
Why would I help a liar
who refuses my hospitality?

782
01:16:47.890 --> 01:16:48.890
Come on.

783
01:16:58.530 --> 01:17:00.790
I remember we were here earlier.

784
01:17:01.090 --> 01:17:02.090
Now they're not.

785
01:17:08.110 --> 01:17:09.510
They moved on?

786
01:17:10.390 --> 01:17:11.390
Yes.

787
01:17:13.730 --> 01:17:15.070
Leaving their rings.

788
01:17:24.110 --> 01:17:25.110
I wouldn't.

789
01:17:26.470 --> 01:17:26.910
Don't.

790
01:17:27.370 --> 01:17:29.050
I don't know who this crow is to you.

791
01:17:29.290 --> 01:17:32.250
If you don't put that knife down.
I will throw you out of it to find her.

792
01:17:32.730 --> 01:17:33.450
Don't hurt her.

793
01:17:33.690 --> 01:17:34.690
Family.

794
01:17:36.710 --> 01:17:37.710
Mother.

795
01:17:40.150 --> 01:17:41.150
Your sister.

796
01:17:44.790 --> 01:17:47.490
You did this to her like you
turned my men into pigs.

797
01:17:47.770 --> 01:17:49.407
I didn't turn your men into anything.

798
01:17:49.431 --> 01:17:50.750
Look at them.

799
01:17:51.050 --> 01:17:52.050
This is who they are.

800
01:17:52.190 --> 01:17:53.190
Look at them.

801
01:17:53.550 --> 01:17:54.550
Base.

802
01:17:54.770 --> 01:17:55.770
Disgusting.

803
01:17:55.870 --> 01:17:57.970
The primal urges that you value in battle.

804
01:17:58.690 --> 01:18:01.170
They've raped and pillaged
across the world for you.

805
01:18:01.530 --> 01:18:03.126
And you think that's against their nature?

806
01:18:03.150 --> 01:18:05.470
You think they only did that
because you ordered them too?

807
01:18:05.710 --> 01:18:08.990
A true commander thinks his
men's savagery is actually discipline.

808
01:18:10.230 --> 01:18:11.710
If you don't seem that deluded.

809
01:18:12.410 --> 01:18:13.990
But you sent them to me here.

810
01:18:14.250 --> 01:18:15.250
On my own.

811
01:18:15.870 --> 01:18:19.350
A gang of filthy soldiers with
empty bellies and hot blood.

812
01:18:21.270 --> 01:18:24.170
You thought they'd respect me and my home?

813
01:18:25.510 --> 01:18:26.510
Look at them.

814
01:18:26.810 --> 01:18:28.010
Know your own men.

815
01:18:29.210 --> 01:18:30.310
Did they hurt you?

816
01:18:31.150 --> 01:18:32.150
They would have.

817
01:18:32.650 --> 01:18:34.390
They just want to go home.

818
01:18:37.810 --> 01:18:38.810
Let them.

819
01:19:31.014 --> 01:19:32.370
Back into your disguise.

820
01:19:38.640 --> 01:19:39.640
Odysseus.

821
01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:41.120
Back to the ship.

822
01:19:41.620 --> 01:19:43.120
Take all the food you can carry.

823
01:19:43.980 --> 01:19:44.980
Leave the meat.

824
01:19:45.395 --> 01:19:46.516
You're not gonna let her live.

825
01:19:46.540 --> 01:19:47.540
She's a witch.

826
01:19:48.025 --> 01:19:49.025
Who gave her powers?

827
01:19:49.700 --> 01:19:50.700
Your gods.

828
01:19:51.070 --> 01:19:52.590
Fill the jugs and skins of the stream.

829
01:19:52.885 --> 01:19:54.000
Be ready to sail at dawn.

830
01:20:00.265 --> 01:20:01.400
Why leave her like this?

831
01:20:02.300 --> 01:20:03.580
We get on better this way.

832
01:20:05.360 --> 01:20:07.000
I'm sorry if we offended you.

833
01:20:08.620 --> 01:20:10.140
We can't get back without your help.

834
01:20:11.060 --> 01:20:12.437
We need to know the
way to the trade routes.

835
01:20:12.461 --> 01:20:13.880
You're too lost.

836
01:20:14.720 --> 01:20:15.720
What do you mean?

837
01:20:17.060 --> 01:20:19.880
Only blind Tiresias can
tell you how to go home.

838
01:20:20.540 --> 01:20:20.760
Tiresias?

839
01:20:21.089 --> 01:20:22.089
In Hades.

840
01:20:22.960 --> 01:20:25.800
Nobody sails into hell. then comes back.

841
01:20:26.205 --> 01:20:27.205
You will.

842
01:20:28.320 --> 01:20:30.500
Head north into the twilight.

843
01:20:36.200 --> 01:20:40.840
And head inland until a
river of fire meets a river of ice.

844
01:21:31.590 --> 01:21:33.290
There you must dig a trench.

845
01:21:35.545 --> 01:21:38.270
And fill it with the blood of sacrifice.

846
01:21:58.500 --> 01:21:59.600
Gather the dead.

847
01:22:01.190 --> 01:22:02.860
And learn how you go home.

848
01:22:05.040 --> 01:22:06.040
You.

849
01:22:07.070 --> 01:22:08.280
Not your men.

850
01:22:09.415 --> 01:22:10.415
You'd curse them?

851
01:22:11.080 --> 01:22:12.080
Not a curse.

852
01:22:13.125 --> 01:22:14.125
A truth.

853
01:22:15.700 --> 01:22:17.920
Your men don't want you to defy the gods.

854
01:22:20.025 --> 01:22:22.120
The gods say only you make it home.

855
01:22:23.480 --> 01:22:24.660
Then I defy the gods.

856
01:23:06.860 --> 01:23:07.860
Sinon.

857
01:23:08.140 --> 01:23:09.140
It's Odysseus.

858
01:23:14.410 --> 01:23:15.410
Sinon.

859
01:23:16.310 --> 01:23:17.310
It's Odysseus.

860
01:23:19.690 --> 01:23:21.570
Let him drink Odysseus.

861
01:23:22.650 --> 01:23:24.030
Then you will hear his testament.

862
01:23:39.050 --> 01:23:40.050
Odysseus?

863
01:23:40.330 --> 01:23:41.330
Yes.

864
01:23:41.710 --> 01:23:43.250
You lied to everyone.

865
01:23:47.050 --> 01:23:48.610
Did you have to lie to me?

866
01:23:50.350 --> 01:23:51.550
I needed you to believe.

867
01:23:53.250 --> 01:23:55.090
Why did you have to volunteer?

868
01:23:56.190 --> 01:23:57.750
Don't you remember how I came here?

869
01:23:57.950 --> 01:24:00.610
I begged to take Antinous' place.

870
01:24:01.230 --> 01:24:02.230
He'd been called.

871
01:24:03.030 --> 01:24:05.690
He offered money for your
family if you'd take his place.

872
01:24:06.985 --> 01:24:08.890
If the Trojans don't let me live.

873
01:24:10.740 --> 01:24:12.310
If I never make it home.

874
01:24:13.430 --> 01:24:14.630
Take this back to my father.

875
01:24:16.520 --> 01:24:18.010
Tell him everything I did.

876
01:24:21.120 --> 01:24:22.120
Odysseus.

877
01:24:23.770 --> 01:24:25.970
Feel free to add some stuff.

878
01:24:29.235 --> 01:24:31.460
You didn't trust me with the truth?

879
01:24:33.380 --> 01:24:35.160
You knew I'd die for you.

880
01:24:35.680 --> 01:24:36.920
Dying men tell the truth.

881
01:24:37.060 --> 01:24:38.060
I needed you to believe.

882
01:24:38.760 --> 01:24:41.060
I died for your lie on that beach.

883
01:24:42.025 --> 01:24:43.025
I know.

884
01:24:43.115 --> 01:24:44.497
I was there with you.

885
01:24:44.521 --> 01:24:49.100
I heard you repeat the lie with
your dying breath. winning us the war.

886
01:24:49.820 --> 01:24:52.340
I was right there. inches
from you. smelling your blood.

887
01:24:52.520 --> 01:24:54.640
What comfort could that give me?

888
01:24:55.815 --> 01:24:59.620
Not knowing you were beside
that horse until the dead told me.

889
01:25:00.700 --> 01:25:01.700
Which dead?

890
01:25:02.160 --> 01:25:05.260
A lot of veterans of your
campaigns walk these shores.

891
01:25:07.560 --> 01:25:12.477
The dead meet to lament the
living and greet the newly dead.

892
01:25:12.501 --> 01:25:16.060
My father was the first shade to find me.

893
01:25:16.540 --> 01:25:17.540
To comfort you?

894
01:25:18.120 --> 01:25:19.120
Shades don't comfort.

895
01:25:19.900 --> 01:25:23.260
They want word of the
living who wronged them.

896
01:25:24.220 --> 01:25:31.561
My father had died in poverty. alone.
begging for scraps from people like Antinous.

897
01:25:31.800 --> 01:25:33.180
Antinous cheated you?

898
01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:35.940
As is no doubt cheating you.

899
01:25:36.440 --> 01:25:36.900
How?

900
01:25:37.280 --> 01:25:39.860
Who was looking after 
your wife and son in Ithaca?

901
01:25:40.200 --> 01:25:41.620
My wife is of no concern.

902
01:25:43.160 --> 01:25:45.680
Talk to Agamemnon about homecomings.

903
01:25:47.120 --> 01:25:48.120
Agamemnon's here?

904
01:25:51.020 --> 01:25:54.540
You buried me at Troy
with respect. thank you.

905
01:25:55.254 --> 01:25:57.340
But spare a thought for those you didn't.

906
01:25:57.960 --> 01:25:59.340
It's not always possible.

907
01:26:00.520 --> 01:26:06.620
When you get home. take a ship. head
into the unknown west till you find land.

908
01:26:07.090 --> 01:26:10.540
And there. give sacrifice
and honor your men.

909
01:26:12.670 --> 01:26:14.240
Do you still have the lot?

910
01:26:18.070 --> 01:26:19.070
Of course.

911
01:26:19.750 --> 01:26:22.850
If you make it home. give
Antinous back his shame.

912
01:26:23.470 --> 01:26:24.470
I will.

913
01:26:35.440 --> 01:26:36.440
My lord.

914
01:26:48.460 --> 01:26:49.460
Odysseus.

915
01:26:49.860 --> 01:26:51.060
How are you here?

916
01:26:51.975 --> 01:26:53.760
We won the war you headed for home.

917
01:26:54.960 --> 01:26:55.960
Odysseus.

918
01:26:56.360 --> 01:26:57.880
The most brilliant of all my generals.

919
01:26:59.294 --> 01:27:01.160
He would never be snared as I was.

920
01:27:01.320 --> 01:27:02.900
Were they cast adrift like us?

921
01:27:03.700 --> 01:27:04.700
No.

922
01:27:04.865 --> 01:27:06.740
My misfortune was to make it home.

923
01:27:08.140 --> 01:27:09.900
To be welcomed by my beautiful wife.

924
01:27:16.470 --> 01:27:17.470
Bathed.

925
01:27:19.910 --> 01:27:20.910
Anointed.

926
01:27:23.330 --> 01:27:24.330
Made soft.

927
01:27:25.510 --> 01:27:30.330
To learn the hardest lesson that I'd
left to poison in her hard years before.

928
01:27:33.070 --> 01:27:35.050
Good part of her heart had gone…

929
01:27:38.330 --> 01:27:39.330
No.

930
01:27:41.390 --> 01:27:44.150
She and her lover had
plotted my murder for years.

931
01:27:45.870 --> 01:27:46.870
Odysseus.

932
01:27:47.419 --> 01:27:48.419
Yes.

933
01:27:48.870 --> 01:27:50.130
My son Orestes.

934
01:27:50.910 --> 01:27:51.970
Did he avenge me?

935
01:27:52.670 --> 01:27:53.710
My lord. how could I know?

936
01:27:54.905 --> 01:27:56.250
I'm still trying to get home.

937
01:27:57.790 --> 01:27:58.790
Don't make my mistake.

938
01:28:00.345 --> 01:28:03.090
Don't walk in the front door
expecting garlands and praise.

939
01:28:04.270 --> 01:28:05.270
Come in disguise.

940
01:28:06.144 --> 01:28:07.144
Take your time.

941
01:28:08.285 --> 01:28:09.285
Assess.

942
01:28:16.680 --> 01:28:17.680
Do you see?

943
01:28:19.120 --> 01:28:20.660
I know what I have to do.

944
01:28:21.300 --> 01:28:22.640
Some of what you have to do.

945
01:28:22.980 --> 01:28:24.760
My prophecy is not for their ears.

946
01:28:26.440 --> 01:28:27.440
Back to the ship.

947
01:28:30.060 --> 01:28:32.260
Sail southwest. past the sirens.

948
01:28:33.769 --> 01:28:36.020
Their song lures your men onto the rocks.

949
01:28:36.285 --> 01:28:37.285
They'll all die.

950
01:28:37.375 --> 01:28:38.400
I won't let that happen.

951
01:28:39.300 --> 01:28:40.300
I'll block their ears.

952
01:28:40.600 --> 01:28:41.600
With wax.

953
01:28:41.815 --> 01:28:42.815
Past the sirens.

954
01:28:43.620 --> 01:28:46.800
Choose behind the sirens between 
the Charybdis whirlpool and the monster Scylla.

955
01:28:47.250 --> 01:28:49.620
The whirlpool in the
sea takes all of your lives.

956
01:28:49.900 --> 01:28:50.940
The monster in the cliffs?

957
01:28:51.059 --> 01:28:52.059
Only six.

958
01:28:52.285 --> 01:28:56.580
But your determination to not sacrifice
any more lives kills all of them.

959
01:28:57.259 --> 01:28:58.680
I'll do what I have to.

960
01:28:58.920 --> 01:29:02.083
At the end. when starvation
and mutiny threaten. they

961
01:29:02.107 --> 01:29:05.000
demand to land where the
sun god grazes his cattle.

962
01:29:05.549 --> 01:29:07.280
They slaughter Apollo's herd.

963
01:29:09.020 --> 01:29:10.360
Zeus kills them for it.

964
01:29:10.559 --> 01:29:11.560
Do you see?

965
01:29:12.600 --> 01:29:13.940
I'll stop them.

966
01:29:14.060 --> 01:29:17.680
Why do you value these men's lives
more than those you've already lost?

967
01:29:17.840 --> 01:29:19.560
I can still save them from the gods.

968
01:29:20.640 --> 01:29:22.220
But not from themselves.

969
01:29:25.020 --> 01:29:26.020
What is that?

970
01:29:26.500 --> 01:29:27.640
Your own buried crew.

971
01:29:31.400 --> 01:29:33.400
Demanding you share their dishonor.

972
01:29:35.680 --> 01:29:36.700
Let's go!

973
01:30:37.410 --> 01:30:38.087
Let's go!

974
01:30:38.111 --> 01:30:46.111
We rode out of hell.

975
01:30:51.320 --> 01:30:53.310
Leaving so many fallen comrades.

976
01:30:54.555 --> 01:30:55.970
I have to save the rest.

977
01:30:57.005 --> 01:30:58.150
What's happened to them?

978
01:30:59.050 --> 01:31:00.330
Did they leave me here?

979
01:31:00.815 --> 01:31:01.830
Are they coming back?

980
01:31:04.350 --> 01:31:05.810
Did you do something to them?

981
01:31:07.600 --> 01:31:08.990
Did you do something to them?

982
01:31:09.330 --> 01:31:10.330
Have some.

983
01:31:10.780 --> 01:31:12.710
You told me not to eat the lotus flower.

984
01:31:13.070 --> 01:31:14.070
Just tonight.

985
01:31:14.880 --> 01:31:16.230
So you can get some sleep.

986
01:31:19.905 --> 01:31:22.740
And tomorrow. when
you're ready. you'll remember.

987
01:31:26.295 --> 01:31:27.600
You'll remember your crew.

988
01:31:28.660 --> 01:31:29.660
And your family.

989
01:31:29.900 --> 01:31:30.900
Family.

990
01:31:32.920 --> 01:31:33.920
A wife.

991
01:31:35.900 --> 01:31:36.900
Do I have a wife?

992
01:31:43.000 --> 01:31:44.010
So I'm Melanto.

993
01:31:45.025 --> 01:31:46.210
Strangers don't come in here.

994
01:31:46.615 --> 01:31:49.009
Screen or no screen.
after three years of feasting

995
01:31:49.033 --> 01:31:50.950
together. we're far
from strangers. Penelope.

996
01:31:51.155 --> 01:31:53.760
Are you trying to marry
me or ruin my reputation?

997
01:31:54.595 --> 01:31:55.690
Melanto can be discreet.

998
01:31:56.360 --> 01:31:59.510
I'm actually here out of
concern for your reputation.

999
01:31:59.950 --> 01:32:00.950
Oh?

1000
01:32:01.650 --> 01:32:02.690
Please. how hard you work.

1001
01:32:02.800 --> 01:32:05.050
Slow. steady progress.

1002
01:32:06.550 --> 01:32:08.630
But every night. you've
been unraveling that work.

1003
01:32:14.740 --> 01:32:15.740
Leave us.

1004
01:32:17.195 --> 01:32:21.060
You can't expect her to be loyal
to a master she never even knew.

1005
01:32:21.415 --> 01:32:23.040
I expect her to be loyal to me.

1006
01:32:23.214 --> 01:32:24.214
Then she will be.

1007
01:32:24.575 --> 01:32:25.575
When you marry me.

1008
01:32:28.240 --> 01:32:30.320
Slaves have no choice but
to be loyal to the future.

1009
01:32:30.725 --> 01:32:32.700
This is a household waiting for a master.

1010
01:32:33.160 --> 01:32:35.160
Just as Ithaca. just as a kingdom 
waiting for a king.

1011
01:32:37.985 --> 01:32:39.480
Ithaca's king is coming back.

1012
01:32:40.135 --> 01:32:41.135
No. he's not.

1013
01:32:43.010 --> 01:32:45.600
Did you know that as a boy.
he tried to go with him to Troy?

1014
01:32:47.000 --> 01:32:48.020
He never told me that.

1015
01:32:49.815 --> 01:32:51.020
Odysseus refused to let me.

1016
01:32:51.420 --> 01:32:54.740
He knew that Ithaca
needed a future without him.

1017
01:33:01.360 --> 01:33:02.960
And he saw me as part of that future.

1018
01:33:08.140 --> 01:33:10.310
I'll never tell other suitors about you.

1019
01:33:11.970 --> 01:33:12.970
Deception.

1020
01:33:15.820 --> 01:33:16.820
Finish your weaving.

1021
01:33:19.140 --> 01:33:20.140
It's time to let him go.

1022
01:33:29.875 --> 01:33:32.235
Why would you want to marry
someone who loves someone else?

1023
01:33:35.445 --> 01:33:39.750
Your loyalty to Odysseus is
one of your finest qualities.

1024
01:33:43.485 --> 01:33:44.485
I love you.

1025
01:33:45.455 --> 01:33:48.330
I want you to choose me and end all this.

1026
01:33:50.170 --> 01:33:52.250
You can't tell me you're not tired.

1027
01:33:53.350 --> 01:33:54.350
The waiting.

1028
01:33:54.815 --> 01:33:55.815
The uncertainty.

1029
01:33:58.215 --> 01:34:00.490
You've done your duty.

1030
01:34:02.030 --> 01:34:05.270
Year after year after year.

1031
01:34:08.460 --> 01:34:09.700
It's time to think of yourself.

1032
01:34:11.940 --> 01:34:12.970
It's time to live again.

1033
01:34:18.880 --> 01:34:20.400
It's what Odysseus would have wanted.

1034
01:34:27.320 --> 01:34:28.690
I'll take care of all your needs.

1035
01:34:33.860 --> 01:34:34.550
My queen.

1036
01:34:37.690 --> 01:34:38.690
And my son?

1037
01:34:40.365 --> 01:34:41.690
The throne is his.

1038
01:34:42.315 --> 01:34:43.315
He's the heir.

1039
01:34:45.790 --> 01:34:47.410
Unless we have a child of our own.

1040
01:34:57.235 --> 01:34:58.660
Where is Telemachus?

1041
01:35:00.510 --> 01:35:01.840
He's gone inland.

1042
01:35:02.625 --> 01:35:03.640
To visit his grandfather.

1043
01:35:51.305 --> 01:35:52.530
I was coming for Argus.

1044
01:35:54.125 --> 01:35:55.450
I told you that dog stays there.

1045
01:35:55.450 --> 01:35:56.870
Go back to the farm.

1046
01:35:56.870 --> 01:35:58.610
When we need your
pigs. we'll send Melantius.

1047
01:35:59.905 --> 01:36:02.150
You're no longer welcome
in this palace. old man.

1048
01:36:02.245 --> 01:36:03.910
I answer to my mistress.

1049
01:36:06.450 --> 01:36:07.690
Does she even know your name?

1050
01:36:09.470 --> 01:36:10.470
Now go!

1051
01:36:11.510 --> 01:36:14.450
Telemachus will have to come
back by the temple at Pylos.

1052
01:36:15.350 --> 01:36:16.710
You take your man away from there.

1053
01:36:17.430 --> 01:36:19.466
Why. so you can stay and
try and turn on the queen?

1054
01:36:19.490 --> 01:36:20.490
I don't think so.

1055
01:36:21.200 --> 01:36:22.610
He needs to die on that road.

1056
01:36:23.050 --> 01:36:24.530
And Eris has a plan.

1057
01:36:25.990 --> 01:36:26.990
Eris!

1058
01:36:28.785 --> 01:36:32.830
I need everyone in his party
dead. in a ditch. throats open.

1059
01:36:33.470 --> 01:36:34.958
Telemachus and his
men will be ready for the

1060
01:36:34.982 --> 01:36:37.731
bandits who work the road
between here and Pylos.

1061
01:36:38.550 --> 01:36:41.830
But if some of them took the temple
from the priests before they arrived…

1062
01:36:43.655 --> 01:36:45.270
Then bring me part of Telemachus' proof.

1063
01:36:47.010 --> 01:36:48.010
His nose.

1064
01:36:48.855 --> 01:36:50.350
Would you know him only by his nose?

1065
01:36:53.980 --> 01:36:55.360
His ears too.

1066
01:37:00.440 --> 01:37:01.550
Who taught you?

1067
01:37:02.095 --> 01:37:03.610
My father's swineherd.

1068
01:37:04.170 --> 01:37:05.170
Swineherd?

1069
01:37:05.205 --> 01:37:06.365
Your best most loyal servant.

1070
01:37:07.410 --> 01:37:09.910
Odysseus left his most
loyal servant behind.

1071
01:37:10.485 --> 01:37:11.630
He always was the smartest.

1072
01:37:12.905 --> 01:37:14.870
Only one Agamemnon I never listened to.

1073
01:37:16.130 --> 01:37:17.250
Should have listened harder.

1074
01:37:18.110 --> 01:37:19.710
Can I ask what happened to your brother?

1075
01:37:19.790 --> 01:37:21.970
His wife betrayed him and murdered him.

1076
01:37:23.010 --> 01:37:24.010
What did you do to him?

1077
01:37:24.040 --> 01:37:26.050
My son Orestes. he killed them both.

1078
01:37:26.955 --> 01:37:28.115
But he killed his own mother?

1079
01:37:29.470 --> 01:37:30.670
He avenged his father.

1080
01:37:31.715 --> 01:37:32.835
But you don't have to worry.

1081
01:37:33.150 --> 01:37:35.530
Your mother has nothing in
common with Clytemnestra.

1082
01:37:36.230 --> 01:37:37.230
Maybe not.

1083
01:37:37.470 --> 01:37:40.110
But the suitors have convinced
the elders it's time she remarried.

1084
01:37:41.220 --> 01:37:42.870
It's not a good time for an empty throne.

1085
01:37:43.750 --> 01:37:45.630
Heard the stories. people from the sea.

1086
01:37:46.295 --> 01:37:46.990
Have you been raided?

1087
01:37:47.129 --> 01:37:49.610
Not yet. but the attacks.
they do seem closer.

1088
01:37:49.969 --> 01:37:50.969
Sparta's ready.

1089
01:37:51.299 --> 01:37:52.299
What about Ithaca?

1090
01:37:53.890 --> 01:37:55.050
The army defends itself.

1091
01:37:55.310 --> 01:37:56.630
Someone's got to take the throne.

1092
01:37:56.735 --> 01:37:58.850
Then the battle is there. not here. son.

1093
01:37:59.910 --> 01:38:01.170
You've run from the problem.

1094
01:38:02.520 --> 01:38:04.670
Left your mother at the mercy of the suitors.

1095
01:38:06.290 --> 01:38:07.350
Should I hire mercenaries?

1096
01:38:08.215 --> 01:38:09.390
Go back and kill them all?

1097
01:38:10.185 --> 01:38:12.930
Murder rich and powerful
guests in your own home.

1098
01:38:13.370 --> 01:38:14.370
You'll be exiled.

1099
01:38:15.250 --> 01:38:16.250
What then?

1100
01:38:16.525 --> 01:38:19.070
You must evict them
without violating Zeus' law.

1101
01:38:21.450 --> 01:38:22.810
I have to take the throne.

1102
01:38:23.970 --> 01:38:25.870
I have to know if Odysseus is alive.

1103
01:38:26.930 --> 01:38:29.170
This has nothing to do with
a father you've never known.

1104
01:38:30.330 --> 01:38:31.370
You have to lead. son.

1105
01:38:32.030 --> 01:38:33.550
No matter what's happened to Odysseus.

1106
01:38:47.560 --> 01:38:49.020
You need to remember.

1107
01:38:49.315 --> 01:38:50.315
No. I don't.

1108
01:38:51.060 --> 01:38:52.580
I don't need to know the truth.

1109
01:38:52.720 --> 01:38:54.060
I'm happy here with you.

1110
01:38:56.475 --> 01:39:00.360
And why are you gathering the
bones of your old ship to make a raft?

1111
01:39:02.920 --> 01:39:04.080
Remember your crew.

1112
01:39:06.020 --> 01:39:07.020
Your journey.

1113
01:39:11.280 --> 01:39:13.000
I heard the song of the sirens.

1114
01:39:22.840 --> 01:39:24.460
Put the wax in the men's ears.

1115
01:39:25.840 --> 01:39:26.400
None for me.

1116
01:39:26.780 --> 01:39:27.780
Tie me to the mast.

1117
01:39:28.000 --> 01:39:28.260
Why?

1118
01:39:28.760 --> 01:39:30.140
I want to hear the siren's song.

1119
01:39:30.720 --> 01:39:31.720
No.

1120
01:39:31.820 --> 01:39:32.820
That's a bad idea.

1121
01:39:33.580 --> 01:39:35.116
No one's ever heard the song and lived.

1122
01:39:35.140 --> 01:39:36.260
That's why it's a good idea.

1123
01:39:36.340 --> 01:39:37.340
I'll be the first.

1124
01:39:38.300 --> 01:39:42.180
No matter how hard I plead. do
not untie me until we're well clear.

1125
01:40:46.900 --> 01:40:48.240
What was the siren's song?

1126
01:40:49.880 --> 01:40:51.450
All the things you wanted to be.

1127
01:40:52.340 --> 01:40:56.010
Then. all the things you
wish you never wished for.

1128
01:41:13.680 --> 01:41:16.840
It was the delicious
itch you go to scratch.

1129
01:41:18.540 --> 01:41:21.280
They find it's under the
skin and you can't reach it.

1130
01:41:23.140 --> 01:41:25.720
So the delicious itch becomes unbearable.

1131
01:41:29.960 --> 01:41:36.400
I told you what you most want
is what you most can't have.

1132
01:41:39.870 --> 01:41:46.310
And what you most can't have
is what you already had and lost.

1133
01:41:55.180 --> 01:41:57.860
It was the song of all the
promises I failed to keep.

1134
01:42:02.950 --> 01:42:05.770
And it told me I don't
really want to go home.

1135
01:42:10.180 --> 01:42:11.640
My water ahead!

1136
01:42:14.440 --> 01:42:15.440
Charybdis.

1137
01:42:16.780 --> 01:42:18.140
The monstrous whirlpool.

1138
01:42:21.240 --> 01:42:22.240
Hoist the sails!

1139
01:42:22.560 --> 01:42:23.560
Come on. let's go!

1140
01:42:24.020 --> 01:42:25.220
Get the whirlpool shot!

1141
01:42:26.040 --> 01:42:27.040
Can we head out to sea?

1142
01:42:27.160 --> 01:42:28.200
It's already got us.

1143
01:42:30.600 --> 01:42:31.600
Get in the boat!

1144
01:42:32.280 --> 01:42:33.380
The water hammer is moving!

1145
01:42:34.980 --> 01:42:36.340
We might make that canyon.

1146
01:42:36.520 --> 01:42:37.520
It's too narrow.

1147
01:42:38.040 --> 01:42:39.040
Make for the whirlpool.

1148
01:42:39.560 --> 01:42:43.180
If you steer down the slope. we can make
enough weight to shoot out the other side.

1149
01:42:43.620 --> 01:42:45.060
Don't be afraid. we can do it.

1150
01:42:50.210 --> 01:42:51.210
No.

1151
01:42:51.290 --> 01:42:52.290
No!

1152
01:42:52.590 --> 01:42:53.750
We're making for the channel.

1153
01:42:54.290 --> 01:42:55.510
We're making for the channel!

1154
01:42:56.170 --> 01:42:57.170
Oars in!

1155
01:42:58.790 --> 01:43:00.250
Get the oars in!

1156
01:43:00.710 --> 01:43:01.710
It's over an oars!

1157
01:43:02.010 --> 01:43:03.190
Prepare to hold water!

1158
01:43:10.230 --> 01:43:11.700
On my signal!

1159
01:43:13.220 --> 01:43:14.060
Three…

1160
01:43:15.890 --> 01:43:16.950
Two...

1161
01:43:18.430 --> 01:43:18.980
One...

1162
01:43:19.220 --> 01:43:20.010
Hard!

1163
01:43:20.940 --> 01:43:21.940
Three!

1164
01:44:07.325 --> 01:44:09.360
We made it!

1165
01:44:10.120 --> 01:44:11.720
We did it!

1166
01:44:11.900 --> 01:44:13.061
We did it!

1167
01:44:13.085 --> 01:44:14.085
You guys…

1168
01:44:23.960 --> 01:44:25.800
Keep us straight!

1169
01:44:32.950 --> 01:44:34.890
Keep us straight!

1170
01:45:01.450 --> 01:45:02.450
You knew!

1171
01:45:03.450 --> 01:45:04.490
And you didn't tell us!

1172
01:45:05.110 --> 01:45:07.390
If I told you. you wouldn't
have chosen this way.

1173
01:45:07.750 --> 01:45:09.970
And those men would still be alive!

1174
01:45:10.350 --> 01:45:10.350
No!

1175
01:45:10.730 --> 01:45:11.730
We'd all be dead!

1176
01:45:12.230 --> 01:45:13.230
You had no right!

1177
01:45:13.465 --> 01:45:14.470
I had every right!

1178
01:45:14.910 --> 01:45:16.370
And every responsibility!

1179
01:45:17.030 --> 01:45:19.730
The whirlpool would have
destroyed the ship and all of us!

1180
01:45:20.190 --> 01:45:21.190
This way we live!

1181
01:45:21.410 --> 01:45:22.410
Some of us live!

1182
01:45:22.550 --> 01:45:23.550
Yes!

1183
01:45:26.730 --> 01:45:28.610
What else did Tiresias tell you?

1184
01:45:40.300 --> 01:45:42.360
I'm going to put in there…

1185
01:45:44.160 --> 01:45:45.820
We can't land there!

1186
01:45:46.020 --> 01:45:47.420
That's the sun god's isle!

1187
01:45:47.880 --> 01:45:48.880
We need repairs!

1188
01:45:50.300 --> 01:45:51.780
These men are exhausted!

1189
01:45:52.380 --> 01:45:53.480
I want six shipmates!

1190
01:45:54.220 --> 01:45:56.700
I'm going to put in… I'm
going to rest up on dry land.

1191
01:45:57.060 --> 01:45:58.120
You're all going to die!

1192
01:46:01.320 --> 01:46:03.360
You asked me what Tiresias told me.

1193
01:46:04.330 --> 01:46:05.660
He said you're all going to die.

1194
01:46:06.260 --> 01:46:13.261
If you slaughter the sun god's cattle
which graze on that island. you'll all die.

1195
01:46:16.760 --> 01:46:17.790
That's fine.

1196
01:46:18.475 --> 01:46:19.830
We'll put in.

1197
01:46:20.980 --> 01:46:22.230
We'll cook our own provisions.

1198
01:46:22.535 --> 01:46:23.310
We'll pick fruit.

1199
01:46:23.450 --> 01:46:24.070
We'll get water.

1200
01:46:24.430 --> 01:46:25.430
Nothing more.

1201
01:46:26.770 --> 01:46:27.770
Swear to it.

1202
01:46:28.470 --> 01:46:29.470
All of you.

1203
01:46:43.290 --> 01:46:44.290
See?

1204
01:46:45.550 --> 01:46:46.930
The wind took us right in.

1205
01:46:48.170 --> 01:46:49.610
We'll rest up…

1206
01:46:50.110 --> 01:46:52.910
Do the repairs and set out
as soon as the wind changes.

1207
01:46:54.290 --> 01:46:55.290
Grab that light!

1208
01:47:06.130 --> 01:47:07.790
Did the crew keep their promise?

1209
01:47:09.330 --> 01:47:10.330
Yes.

1210
01:47:10.890 --> 01:47:12.190
For a long time.

1211
01:47:14.005 --> 01:47:15.590
But the wind never changed.

1212
01:47:16.050 --> 01:47:21.567
The steady onshore breeze
that made it so easy for us to land

1213
01:47:21.591 --> 01:47:23.770
made it impossible to leave.

1214
01:47:27.880 --> 01:47:29.680
And our provisions ran out.

1215
01:47:32.860 --> 01:47:33.860
Go!

1216
01:48:36.300 --> 01:48:39.420
Did you think if you ate quietly
the sun god wouldn't notice?

1217
01:48:40.200 --> 01:48:41.440
We thought you wouldn't notice.

1218
01:48:42.415 --> 01:48:43.500
I'm not the problem.

1219
01:48:43.945 --> 01:48:44.960
I wanted you to live.

1220
01:48:51.200 --> 01:48:52.220
Tiresias told you.

1221
01:48:52.800 --> 01:48:53.460
Cersei told you.

1222
01:48:53.540 --> 01:48:54.540
The gods told you.

1223
01:48:55.620 --> 01:48:57.640
You gods don't speak
in ways we understand.

1224
01:48:58.060 --> 01:48:59.380
Who doesn't understand pain?

1225
01:49:00.320 --> 01:49:01.660
Or blood or death?

1226
01:49:02.360 --> 01:49:05.500
When you watch your men die. do you
see chance or do you see consequence?

1227
01:49:06.560 --> 01:49:07.560
I see consequence.

1228
01:49:07.960 --> 01:49:08.960
I see my failure.

1229
01:49:09.780 --> 01:49:11.216
What if their deaths aren't your failure?

1230
01:49:11.240 --> 01:49:14.280
I lead them.

1231
01:49:15.900 --> 01:49:16.960
I led them here.

1232
01:49:17.880 --> 01:49:18.880
And who led you?

1233
01:49:20.240 --> 01:49:21.800
Why do you put yourself above the gods?

1234
01:49:21.940 --> 01:49:23.320
Why do you claim responsibility?

1235
01:49:24.640 --> 01:49:25.960
Why don't you want to go home?

1236
01:49:26.510 --> 01:49:27.510
What do you expect?

1237
01:49:29.255 --> 01:49:30.800
They ran out of food!

1238
01:49:31.935 --> 01:49:33.000
Have some pity.

1239
01:49:33.180 --> 01:49:35.980
My pity is what put
us on this damn island.

1240
01:49:36.320 --> 01:49:37.480
I told you what would happen.

1241
01:49:37.800 --> 01:49:38.800
Yes. you did.

1242
01:49:39.240 --> 01:49:42.060
And we decided we would
rather drown than starve.

1243
01:49:48.040 --> 01:49:49.860
Well. now's our chance.

1244
01:49:50.385 --> 01:49:51.460
The wind has dropped!

1245
01:49:51.670 --> 01:49:52.680
The wind has dropped!

1246
01:49:56.040 --> 01:50:00.080
The calm was just long enough to
lure us out onto Poseidon's ocean.

1247
01:50:01.670 --> 01:50:02.670
Under Zeus' sky.

1248
01:50:04.380 --> 01:50:05.680
Stay off!

1249
01:50:06.520 --> 01:50:07.520
It's too late!

1250
01:50:08.000 --> 01:50:09.000
Hurry go!

1251
01:50:10.840 --> 01:50:11.840
Hurry go!

1252
01:50:13.320 --> 01:50:14.380
Hold on!

1253
01:50:21.160 --> 01:50:24.240
The wind has just stopped!

1254
01:50:25.800 --> 01:50:26.800
Hurry!

1255
01:50:28.060 --> 01:50:36.060
My wind!

1256
01:51:54.370 --> 01:51:57.730
The last thing I remember
was the harsh hand of Zeus.

1257
01:52:42.189 --> 01:52:43.770
I don't know what happened to my men.

1258
01:52:43.770 --> 01:52:44.350
Yes you do.

1259
01:52:46.940 --> 01:52:48.520
Exactly what you told them what happened.

1260
01:52:49.410 --> 01:52:51.180
I washed up on your shore alone.

1261
01:52:51.820 --> 01:52:56.220
You washed up on my shore with
some of your boat and none of your crew.

1262
01:52:57.960 --> 01:53:00.140
Half dead. unconscious.

1263
01:53:23.160 --> 01:53:28.570
I watched you lost for days into weeks.

1264
01:53:29.870 --> 01:53:33.135
I knew the lotus would
ease your awakening so

1265
01:53:33.159 --> 01:53:36.691
that your pain was more
of the body than the mind.

1266
01:53:38.970 --> 01:53:40.110
And it did.

1267
01:53:43.190 --> 01:53:44.470
You liked it so much.

1268
01:53:45.745 --> 01:53:47.650
You ate it so willingly.

1269
01:53:51.730 --> 01:53:52.950
And as I cared for you.

1270
01:53:53.650 --> 01:53:54.650
Thank you.

1271
01:53:55.035 --> 01:53:56.130
I came to love you.

1272
01:54:01.835 --> 01:54:03.200
How long have I been here?

1273
01:54:05.785 --> 01:54:08.300
I kept telling myself
to stop giving it to you.

1274
01:54:08.510 --> 01:54:10.180
That you were strong enough to remember.

1275
01:54:10.440 --> 01:54:11.316
How long. Calypso?

1276
01:54:11.340 --> 01:54:12.580
But you were so happy.

1277
01:54:12.820 --> 01:54:13.500
You were happy.

1278
01:54:13.660 --> 01:54:15.180
We were so happy.

1279
01:54:15.530 --> 01:54:16.530
For years.

1280
01:54:16.840 --> 01:54:17.840
Years?

1281
01:54:24.085 --> 01:54:25.260
Seven years.

1282
01:54:26.610 --> 01:54:30.000
You fed me lotus flower
and held me captive.

1283
01:54:30.250 --> 01:54:31.580
I saved your life.

1284
01:54:32.780 --> 01:54:33.800
I rebuilt your body.

1285
01:54:34.000 --> 01:54:36.760
I just couldn't bring
myself to rebuild your mind.

1286
01:54:38.049 --> 01:54:39.440
You have been happy.

1287
01:54:40.615 --> 01:54:43.520
Your heart was just always somewhere else.

1288
01:54:45.020 --> 01:54:48.000
And your mind was
bound to follow eventually.

1289
01:54:49.560 --> 01:54:50.237
How could you?

1290
01:54:50.261 --> 01:54:54.037
The lotus clouds the
mind and stops the will.

1291
01:54:54.061 --> 01:54:56.020
But it doesn't change who you are.

1292
01:54:56.780 --> 01:54:59.560
You weren't ready to go
home for whatever reason.

1293
01:55:00.120 --> 01:55:01.120
I am now.

1294
01:55:02.520 --> 01:55:04.540
As I always knew you would be.

1295
01:55:05.540 --> 01:55:05.700
Now?

1296
01:55:06.220 --> 01:55:08.860
You were clutching
something when I found you.

1297
01:55:09.200 --> 01:55:10.780
Your hand wouldn't open for weeks.

1298
01:55:11.700 --> 01:55:12.860
You'd call out.

1299
01:55:13.880 --> 01:55:17.820
Mostly to Athena. but sometimes
you would say another name.

1300
01:55:37.100 --> 01:55:38.720
Help me go home.

1301
01:55:39.760 --> 01:55:40.760
I am.

1302
01:55:41.680 --> 01:55:44.020
You're a man who
needs to control his fate.

1303
01:55:45.420 --> 01:55:47.160
But you cannot control this.

1304
01:55:50.580 --> 01:55:52.480
You must give yourself to the storm.

1305
01:55:53.460 --> 01:55:54.897
Give yourself to Poseidon.

1306
01:55:54.921 --> 01:55:57.920
And take your punishment.

1307
01:56:00.000 --> 01:56:02.800
I just won't let him kill you.

1308
01:56:05.840 --> 01:56:09.300
Give up the fight.
give up control and live.

1309
01:56:12.220 --> 01:56:16.140
It's a leap of faith you've
never yet made Odysseus.

1310
01:57:08.430 --> 01:57:09.430
Odysseus.

1311
01:57:17.540 --> 01:57:18.540
Where am I?

1312
01:57:20.640 --> 01:57:22.480
Don't you recognize your own home?

1313
01:58:01.630 --> 01:58:03.350
Zeus welcomes you. strangers.

1314
01:58:04.395 --> 01:58:05.710
How many are you?

1315
01:58:06.125 --> 01:58:07.125
Half a dozen.

1316
01:58:07.854 --> 01:58:08.764
In need of a meal?

1317
01:58:09.310 --> 01:58:10.310
And supplies.

1318
01:58:10.870 --> 01:58:11.870
For the road.

1319
01:58:12.570 --> 01:58:13.570
Where are you headed?

1320
01:58:13.770 --> 01:58:14.770
We need pigs.

1321
01:58:15.230 --> 01:58:16.610
We're going all the way to Pylos.

1322
01:58:17.210 --> 01:58:18.210
Are you pilgrims?

1323
01:58:19.390 --> 01:58:20.610
Do we look like pilgrims?

1324
01:58:21.120 --> 01:58:22.190
I don't see well.

1325
01:58:23.590 --> 01:58:27.070
Zeus demands I offer you a meal.
but these pigs aren't mine to sell.

1326
01:58:27.490 --> 01:58:28.490
Oh. don't worry.

1327
01:58:29.110 --> 01:58:30.110
We're not paying.

1328
01:58:33.345 --> 01:58:35.410
Give my greetings to your mother.

1329
01:58:38.925 --> 01:58:39.950
Tell her I'm sorry.

1330
01:58:40.660 --> 01:58:43.386
I'm sorry for Troy. and 
for everything that happened in my name.

1331
01:58:50.851 --> 01:58:52.670
I'm sorry I had no news of your father.

1332
01:58:53.125 --> 01:58:54.125
No. you had news.

1333
01:58:54.800 --> 01:58:56.810
Not of where he is. but who he is.

1334
01:58:58.090 --> 01:59:00.470
And now more than ever. I
hope to one day meet him.

1335
01:59:05.700 --> 01:59:07.480
Where are your watchdogs. old man?

1336
01:59:12.105 --> 01:59:13.760
I'm burning their bodies.

1337
01:59:18.745 --> 01:59:21.027
Can Zeus offer you
food and drink. stranger?

1338
01:59:21.051 --> 01:59:23.070
Were you attacked?

1339
01:59:24.160 --> 01:59:25.550
The dogs saved your farmhands.

1340
01:59:26.075 --> 01:59:28.050
Oh. we burned his brothers first.

1341
01:59:31.350 --> 01:59:32.370
What happened to Ithaca?

1342
01:59:33.389 --> 01:59:34.389
Darkness.

1343
01:59:34.685 --> 01:59:36.950
Zeus's law smashed to pieces.

1344
01:59:37.955 --> 01:59:40.990
A kingdom without a
king since my master died.

1345
01:59:41.415 --> 01:59:42.415
Your master?

1346
01:59:43.520 --> 01:59:44.520
Odysseus.

1347
01:59:45.380 --> 01:59:46.630
Hero of the Trojan War.

1348
01:59:49.290 --> 01:59:50.550
But Odysseus is alive.

1349
01:59:50.995 --> 01:59:53.850
Beggars with false hopes!
No more!

1350
01:59:54.250 --> 01:59:54.770
No more!

1351
01:59:55.145 --> 01:59:56.150
No more!

1352
01:59:59.330 --> 02:00:00.330
Telemachus.

1353
02:00:01.470 --> 02:00:02.470
Telemachus.

1354
02:00:04.760 --> 02:00:06.630
He's in danger and I can't help him.

1355
02:00:07.035 --> 02:00:10.490
There must be others in Ithaca
that would help Odysseus's son.

1356
02:00:10.730 --> 02:00:12.710
How is this controlled by suitors?

1357
02:00:13.575 --> 02:00:15.890
No one is still loyal to Odysseus.

1358
02:00:17.310 --> 02:00:18.310
What about the queen?

1359
02:00:20.080 --> 02:00:21.210
Beautiful. wise Penelope.

1360
02:00:22.360 --> 02:00:24.590
Held hostage by the worst men.

1361
02:00:25.020 --> 02:00:26.930
Soon to be forced into marriage.

1362
02:00:27.090 --> 02:00:29.110
What could she do without even her son?

1363
02:00:29.615 --> 02:00:30.615
Here. drink.

1364
02:00:35.790 --> 02:00:37.170
What's the danger to Telemachus?

1365
02:00:38.310 --> 02:00:40.630
He's on his way back from Sparta.

1366
02:00:41.635 --> 02:00:46.030
The suitors send bandits to
Pylos to ambush him at the temple.

1367
02:00:47.094 --> 02:00:48.170
Give me food for the road.

1368
02:00:49.065 --> 02:00:51.670
You'll never catch up with the bandits.

1369
02:00:52.165 --> 02:00:55.510
If I can get to Pylos before
Telemachus arrives. I can warn him.

1370
02:00:55.895 --> 02:00:57.010
Why are you helping?

1371
02:00:58.240 --> 02:00:59.810
If I tell you. don't be angry.

1372
02:01:00.500 --> 02:01:02.410
I served with Odysseus at Troy.

1373
02:01:02.710 --> 02:01:03.710
Really?

1374
02:01:04.930 --> 02:01:06.310
He was a harsh taskmaster.

1375
02:01:06.930 --> 02:01:07.930
But fair.

1376
02:01:09.180 --> 02:01:11.070
Fair and worthy of following.

1377
02:01:12.660 --> 02:01:13.660
You loved him.

1378
02:01:14.510 --> 02:01:15.510
Like a son?

1379
02:01:16.540 --> 02:01:18.850
Well. he's not dead.

1380
02:01:19.430 --> 02:01:20.970
Is he coming home?

1381
02:01:22.210 --> 02:01:23.210
Soon.

1382
02:01:23.655 --> 02:01:24.655
Bringing vengeance?

1383
02:01:27.225 --> 02:01:28.450
Bringing it all.

1384
02:01:38.120 --> 02:01:39.120
Don't talk.

1385
02:01:39.180 --> 02:01:40.900
We don't want people
realizing who you are.

1386
02:01:41.240 --> 02:01:43.080
Well. maybe we shouldn't
have brought a guard.

1387
02:01:43.300 --> 02:01:45.380
We'll be glad we did if we
have trouble on the road.

1388
02:02:05.420 --> 02:02:07.460
Find that beggar.

1389
02:02:22.640 --> 02:02:24.200
Your men must wait out here.

1390
02:02:24.660 --> 02:02:25.660
The weapons too.

1391
02:02:42.640 --> 02:02:44.010
Zeus welcomes you strangers.

1392
02:02:44.775 --> 02:02:45.775
Have you traveled far?

1393
02:02:46.349 --> 02:02:47.349
Not that far.

1394
02:02:48.705 --> 02:02:50.470
You're here to rest for the night.

1395
02:02:52.630 --> 02:02:54.690
We're here to make sacrifice to Athena.

1396
02:02:56.910 --> 02:02:59.890
And whatever hospitality you
can offer might be considered.

1397
02:03:00.430 --> 02:03:01.430
Of course.

1398
02:03:01.490 --> 02:03:02.650
Get that beggar out of here.

1399
02:03:02.770 --> 02:03:04.150
He has a message for me.

1400
02:03:05.055 --> 02:03:06.250
Did Eumaeus send you?

1401
02:03:07.525 --> 02:03:08.525
Yes. sir.

1402
02:03:14.380 --> 02:03:15.540
But what's the message?

1403
02:03:17.340 --> 02:03:18.340
He's private.

1404
02:03:18.860 --> 02:03:20.000
He's a man of pri…

1405
02:03:21.500 --> 02:03:22.500
He's a man of praise.

1406
02:03:39.540 --> 02:03:42.230
This is your moment to leave. old man.

1407
02:03:43.070 --> 02:03:44.070
Go. stranger.

1408
02:03:46.290 --> 02:03:48.590
I already spared one old man this week.

1409
02:03:49.230 --> 02:03:50.230
But he was blind.

1410
02:03:51.110 --> 02:03:52.110
I won't spare…

1411
02:04:07.710 --> 02:04:08.710
No!

1412
02:04:30.760 --> 02:04:32.340
It's not bad for a beggar.

1413
02:04:33.040 --> 02:04:34.340
No one's born a beggar.

1414
02:04:35.240 --> 02:04:36.480
We'll take this priest's robes.

1415
02:04:37.100 --> 02:04:38.100
Put them over your armor.

1416
02:04:40.300 --> 02:04:41.480
Help me with his body.

1417
02:04:41.820 --> 02:04:42.820
We have to leave him.

1418
02:04:43.300 --> 02:04:43.620
No.

1419
02:04:43.880 --> 02:04:44.960
We have to slip out.

1420
02:04:45.820 --> 02:04:47.180
That's not how you honor the dead.

1421
02:04:47.360 --> 02:04:48.600
That is how you stay alive.

1422
02:04:48.780 --> 02:04:49.780
He was my teacher.

1423
02:04:51.260 --> 02:04:52.756
He might have been Athena in disguise.

1424
02:04:52.780 --> 02:04:53.900
His name was Mentor.

1425
02:04:54.780 --> 02:04:57.380
And when you are safe. we'll
travel to the unknown west.

1426
02:04:58.060 --> 02:04:59.160
And we will honor him.

1427
02:04:59.760 --> 02:05:01.157
These men were sent by the suitors.

1428
02:05:01.181 --> 02:05:03.760
There will be more of them
we need to get on the road.

1429
02:05:04.260 --> 02:05:04.680
By night?

1430
02:05:05.240 --> 02:05:06.280
Safer than staying here.

1431
02:05:06.900 --> 02:05:08.840
Mentor's at the heart of
Athena's oldest temple.

1432
02:05:09.660 --> 02:05:11.640
For now. we'll honor him in flame.

1433
02:05:50.780 --> 02:05:52.420
So how does a beggar come to know Mentor?

1434
02:05:52.630 --> 02:05:53.630
I told you.

1435
02:05:53.840 --> 02:05:55.000
I wasn't always a beggar.

1436
02:05:55.599 --> 02:05:56.840
You have wise eyes.

1437
02:05:58.020 --> 02:05:59.880
Athena's eyes… Don't
look for gods in men.

1438
02:06:00.229 --> 02:06:01.500
You'll just be disappointed.

1439
02:06:01.885 --> 02:06:03.580
Well. then tell me who you are.

1440
02:06:05.410 --> 02:06:06.560
My name is Sinon.

1441
02:06:07.200 --> 02:06:10.140
I'm an old soldier who fought
under your father at Troy.

1442
02:06:11.335 --> 02:06:12.480
You knew my father?

1443
02:06:13.075 --> 02:06:16.080
I've been taking orders from him
for longer than I care to remember.

1444
02:06:17.360 --> 02:06:18.360
Wait. he's alive?

1445
02:06:19.420 --> 02:06:20.420
He's alive.

1446
02:06:22.030 --> 02:06:23.200
Well. where is he?

1447
02:06:23.870 --> 02:06:24.870
He's close by.

1448
02:06:25.550 --> 02:06:27.780
He sent me ahead to assess the situation.

1449
02:06:29.495 --> 02:06:31.140
Well. Mother's not gonna believe it.

1450
02:06:31.275 --> 02:06:32.460
No. you can't tell her.

1451
02:06:33.900 --> 02:06:34.900
What. nothing?

1452
02:06:35.880 --> 02:06:36.880
Tell her.

1453
02:06:38.100 --> 02:06:40.480
Tell her he decided
it's time she remarried.

1454
02:06:42.460 --> 02:06:43.920
He doesn't need to test my mother.

1455
02:06:44.020 --> 02:06:45.020
How can he doubt her?

1456
02:06:45.120 --> 02:06:46.120
He doesn't.

1457
02:06:46.480 --> 02:06:48.760
But she has every reason
to have forgotten him.

1458
02:06:50.575 --> 02:06:52.200
Are there any loyal servants?

1459
02:06:53.700 --> 02:06:54.700
Some.

1460
02:06:55.420 --> 02:06:57.060
Why. Odysseus shows himself all.

1461
02:06:58.940 --> 02:07:00.740
We need to gather
the suitors at the palace.

1462
02:07:01.930 --> 02:07:02.980
That's never a problem.

1463
02:07:03.800 --> 02:07:05.080
You have gifts for Menelaus?

1464
02:07:05.860 --> 02:07:06.860
Yeah. on my ship.

1465
02:07:07.660 --> 02:07:08.660
Good.

1466
02:07:08.980 --> 02:07:09.980
Come back as a prince.

1467
02:07:10.485 --> 02:07:11.485
Call a banquet.

1468
02:07:12.180 --> 02:07:13.180
I'll be there.

1469
02:07:14.035 --> 02:07:15.540
What will you do at the banquet?

1470
02:07:16.700 --> 02:07:17.700
Beg.

1471
02:07:17.915 --> 02:07:19.860
The clearest view of a man is from below.

1472
02:07:21.900 --> 02:07:23.340
How many suitors are there?

1473
02:07:24.500 --> 02:07:25.500
Dozens.

1474
02:07:25.835 --> 02:07:28.680
If all of his men are like you.
then… He doesn't have any men.

1475
02:07:29.815 --> 02:07:30.815
But it's just you?

1476
02:07:31.794 --> 02:07:32.940
And you.

1477
02:07:39.240 --> 02:07:40.440
Telemachus is back.

1478
02:07:40.700 --> 02:07:40.880
What?

1479
02:07:41.020 --> 02:07:42.020
Telemachus is back.

1480
02:07:42.820 --> 02:07:43.820
Who's saying this?

1481
02:07:44.200 --> 02:07:46.660
Everyone's the… Where's Telemachus?

1482
02:07:47.440 --> 02:07:48.440
My queen!

1483
02:07:48.740 --> 02:07:50.120
Surely your place is up there.

1484
02:07:58.340 --> 02:07:59.340
Is it true?

1485
02:08:02.060 --> 02:08:03.140
Leave her home.

1486
02:08:04.200 --> 02:08:05.200
I'm back.

1487
02:08:05.615 --> 02:08:07.340
I thought tonight we could celebrate.

1488
02:08:08.440 --> 02:08:11.360
If we have anything
left to celebrate with.

1489
02:08:12.900 --> 02:08:13.900
Don't worry.

1490
02:08:14.510 --> 02:08:17.340
My brave suitors will shower us
with gifts to honor your return.

1491
02:08:17.880 --> 02:08:19.240
So glad to see your safe return.

1492
02:08:30.230 --> 02:08:31.530
Glad to see you on your feet.

1493
02:08:32.215 --> 02:08:34.110
It's about time we took
you back to the palace.

1494
02:08:38.090 --> 02:08:39.530
What did you hear about your father?

1495
02:08:41.440 --> 02:08:42.440
Nothing.

1496
02:08:43.330 --> 02:08:46.580
A story is from the war. but no one knew
what happened to him after he left Troy.

1497
02:08:47.460 --> 02:08:48.460
I'm sorry. Mom.

1498
02:08:49.575 --> 02:08:52.380
Everyone in Sparta is talking
about a coming catastrophe.

1499
02:08:54.090 --> 02:08:57.860
An age of darkness brought on by
people from the sea. violating Zeus' law.

1500
02:08:58.045 --> 02:08:59.140
Some stories are true.

1501
02:08:59.395 --> 02:09:01.080
Our civilization is collapsing.

1502
02:09:01.705 --> 02:09:04.545
Menelaus is concerned that we can't
raise an army with an empty throne.

1503
02:09:05.120 --> 02:09:06.200
So we came up with a plan.

1504
02:09:07.900 --> 02:09:08.900
What plan?

1505
02:09:11.290 --> 02:09:12.920
The one way to get the suitors to leave.

1506
02:09:15.590 --> 02:09:16.780
It's time for you to choose.

1507
02:09:20.785 --> 02:09:21.970
You want me to remarry?

1508
02:09:22.490 --> 02:09:25.250
If you remarry. the
other suitors will leave.

1509
02:09:25.615 --> 02:09:27.335
And then I'm only
dealing with one of them.

1510
02:09:27.635 --> 02:09:28.635
I'm almost of age.

1511
02:09:28.690 --> 02:09:31.870
I'll tell the elders that I learned
of Odysseus's death in Sparta.

1512
02:09:32.710 --> 02:09:35.550
Menelaus will back me up and
they'll let me assume the throne.

1513
02:09:35.730 --> 02:09:39.130
So you deny your father and I leave my
home and go live with one of these snakes.

1514
02:09:39.310 --> 02:09:40.310
That's your plan.

1515
02:09:40.480 --> 02:09:41.600
Would you have a better one?

1516
02:09:44.285 --> 02:09:47.630
Lock the door. set a fire.
and burn the bastards.

1517
02:09:48.965 --> 02:09:52.410
Do you think if I remarry. life
goes back to the way it was?

1518
02:09:53.670 --> 02:09:55.150
That world is gone.

1519
02:09:55.490 --> 02:09:59.927
And in this one. they're not
giving up power to you or Odysseus.

1520
02:09:59.951 --> 02:10:02.290
If he returned. he'd have to kill him.

1521
02:10:02.450 --> 02:10:03.450
Like you will.

1522
02:10:04.540 --> 02:10:07.430
If I kill them. their families
will demand revenge.

1523
02:10:07.650 --> 02:10:08.650
I'd be exiled.

1524
02:10:09.840 --> 02:10:11.630
And without me. the throne will be lost.

1525
02:10:11.850 --> 02:10:13.490
Because only a man can sit on it.

1526
02:10:15.105 --> 02:10:16.150
Empty throne?

1527
02:10:17.815 --> 02:10:20.690
I've been sitting on that
empty throne for 20 years!

1528
02:10:21.455 --> 02:10:24.508
My knowledge. my years
of experience. that's all

1529
02:10:24.532 --> 02:10:26.891
nothing compared to
the bristles on your chin.

1530
02:10:29.930 --> 02:10:30.930
Do you want peace?

1531
02:10:32.090 --> 02:10:33.090
Or do you want revenge?

1532
02:10:33.270 --> 02:10:33.927
I want Odysseus!

1533
02:10:33.951 --> 02:10:34.951
Dance!

1534
02:10:37.750 --> 02:10:40.550
Sing. muse of a face!

1535
02:10:43.890 --> 02:10:44.910
A face!

1536
02:10:48.910 --> 02:10:50.350
A fleet!

1537
02:10:55.130 --> 02:10:56.130
A war!

1538
02:11:00.430 --> 02:11:01.430
At Troy!

1539
02:11:05.790 --> 02:11:07.410
Sing. muse of a man!

1540
02:11:11.630 --> 02:11:12.630
A man!

1541
02:11:17.870 --> 02:11:18.870
At Troy!

1542
02:11:27.240 --> 02:11:28.740
You may as well go ahead.

1543
02:11:37.040 --> 02:11:38.040
Odysseus.

1544
02:11:59.425 --> 02:12:00.905
You should bury him up on the cliffs.

1545
02:12:02.995 --> 02:12:04.080
Where you met.

1546
02:12:12.780 --> 02:12:14.270
You're Odysseus.

1547
02:12:17.840 --> 02:12:18.840
You're my father.

1548
02:12:21.530 --> 02:12:22.830
I'm a beggar.

1549
02:12:26.300 --> 02:12:27.410
And you

1550
02:12:29.630 --> 02:12:30.710
are the worthy

1551
02:12:32.355 --> 02:12:34.970
beautiful son of Odysseus.

1552
02:12:36.920 --> 02:12:37.920
Of whom

1553
02:12:38.480 --> 02:12:39.830
when you do finally meet.

1554
02:12:42.270 --> 02:12:43.910
He will be so proud.

1555
02:12:48.280 --> 02:12:50.681
Welcome home… stranger.

1556
02:12:53.450 --> 02:12:55.640
Because the darkness of victory

1557
02:12:56.920 --> 02:12:58.380
shrouds the rightest of legends.

1558
02:13:00.360 --> 02:13:03.300
And make bards and blind beggars of us all.

1559
02:13:10.470 --> 02:13:12.740
Accept the hospitality of Zeus.

1560
02:13:13.640 --> 02:13:14.780
Get the strangers some food.

1561
02:13:15.520 --> 02:13:16.520
No. sir.

1562
02:13:17.080 --> 02:13:18.800
I'll just make their ends with a bowl.

1563
02:13:24.520 --> 02:13:27.000
Had to invite another one of
these filthy plate lickers today.

1564
02:13:27.460 --> 02:13:29.040
What's one more beggar at this feast?

1565
02:13:47.460 --> 02:13:50.260
We need to kill him before he ever
gains his position with the elders.

1566
02:13:50.320 --> 02:13:51.320
How?

1567
02:14:09.410 --> 02:14:11.110
Get him to grab one of the weapons.

1568
02:14:15.650 --> 02:14:18.070
Isn't that the same
young pup you used to be?

1569
02:14:18.630 --> 02:14:21.030
When we come back
tonight. let me cut his tongue.

1570
02:14:22.530 --> 02:14:23.530
Fuck off. Olaf.

1571
02:14:23.870 --> 02:14:24.870
Please.

1572
02:14:25.110 --> 02:14:26.246
It's been days since I've eaten.

1573
02:14:26.270 --> 02:14:27.490
I told you to fuck off.

1574
02:14:27.550 --> 02:14:30.390
You can't be generous with
food that's not even yours.

1575
02:14:32.450 --> 02:14:33.890
There's no room in your bowl.

1576
02:14:34.510 --> 02:14:35.590
Yes. there is.

1577
02:14:41.200 --> 02:14:42.200
So there is.

1578
02:14:43.860 --> 02:14:44.960
There you go.

1579
02:14:48.880 --> 02:14:50.860
I suppose you want something from me now.

1580
02:14:51.060 --> 02:14:52.060
No.

1581
02:14:52.480 --> 02:14:54.220
I have something for you.

1582
02:14:55.440 --> 02:14:56.520
You have something from me?

1583
02:14:57.740 --> 02:14:59.040
You have something from me!

1584
02:15:00.720 --> 02:15:07.060
What could a filthy old plate licker
like you possibly have that I could want?

1585
02:15:08.220 --> 02:15:09.900
Actually. it's yours.

1586
02:15:15.300 --> 02:15:17.560
I was asked to return it.

1587
02:15:19.260 --> 02:15:20.400
It's not mine.

1588
02:15:22.460 --> 02:15:23.460
No.

1589
02:15:26.890 --> 02:15:27.890
Where did you get this?

1590
02:15:30.370 --> 02:15:32.330
Don't turn your back on me!

1591
02:15:37.740 --> 02:15:38.740
Continuous.

1592
02:15:39.600 --> 02:15:40.916
You can't treat a beggar like that.

1593
02:15:40.940 --> 02:15:41.940
It's Zeus's law.

1594
02:15:42.880 --> 02:15:43.880
Zeus's law?

1595
02:15:47.720 --> 02:15:49.300
You think this is a god?

1596
02:15:52.600 --> 02:15:54.620
You think this is a god in disguise?

1597
02:15:56.040 --> 02:15:57.040
Are you?

1598
02:15:58.520 --> 02:15:59.640
Are you a god?

1599
02:16:01.400 --> 02:16:02.400
A plate licker?

1600
02:16:03.240 --> 02:16:04.240
No.

1601
02:16:04.780 --> 02:16:06.860
I'm a veteran of the Trojan War.

1602
02:16:08.180 --> 02:16:09.580
A veteran of the Trojan War.

1603
02:16:11.780 --> 02:16:13.720
And what's your name. veteran?

1604
02:16:18.620 --> 02:16:19.620
Sinon.

1605
02:16:22.485 --> 02:16:24.540
Does that name mean something to you?

1606
02:16:25.760 --> 02:16:26.760
No.

1607
02:16:27.880 --> 02:16:31.800
I use it to honor the bravest
young man I ever met.

1608
02:16:32.620 --> 02:16:33.620
Sinon?

1609
02:16:34.985 --> 02:16:36.420
Wasn't he the shepherd's boy?

1610
02:16:39.300 --> 02:16:40.456
I never remember that.

1611
02:16:40.480 --> 02:16:42.840
Yeah. yeah. he wanted
you to take his place.

1612
02:16:43.840 --> 02:16:44.840
Where does he find this?

1613
02:16:45.440 --> 02:16:46.620
Where he'll soon be.

1614
02:16:47.780 --> 02:16:48.780
And where is that?

1615
02:16:50.120 --> 02:16:51.120
Hades.

1616
02:16:54.280 --> 02:16:55.280
Enough!

1617
02:16:56.900 --> 02:16:58.460
The feast is done!

1618
02:17:02.750 --> 02:17:03.430
Let's go.

1619
02:17:04.520 --> 02:17:06.561
That's not mine!

1620
02:17:15.160 --> 02:17:17.240
My queen would like to speak with you.

1621
02:17:17.720 --> 02:17:21.300
Stay till everyone's
left in the fires of the…

1622
02:18:10.940 --> 02:18:11.940
Hello. traveler.

1623
02:18:13.130 --> 02:18:14.130
My queen.

1624
02:18:14.689 --> 02:18:15.689
Melanthal?

1625
02:18:16.045 --> 02:18:18.010
Have Eurykleia washed the stranger's feet?

1626
02:18:18.640 --> 02:18:19.650
That's not necessary.

1627
02:18:19.915 --> 02:18:23.330
My husband insisted we
honor guests as family.

1628
02:18:24.295 --> 02:18:25.295
You know of a deceased?

1629
02:18:25.570 --> 02:18:26.570
My husband?

1630
02:18:26.770 --> 02:18:27.770
Of course.

1631
02:18:28.370 --> 02:18:29.370
Who are the souls?

1632
02:18:32.410 --> 02:18:34.030
And the songs probably seem silly.

1633
02:18:35.910 --> 02:18:36.910
They make me cry.

1634
02:18:38.110 --> 02:18:39.110
Why?

1635
02:18:40.060 --> 02:18:41.060
For what was lost.

1636
02:18:42.310 --> 02:18:43.310
The lives?

1637
02:18:44.070 --> 02:18:45.070
And the years.

1638
02:18:46.550 --> 02:18:47.550
Everything.

1639
02:18:55.379 --> 02:18:56.750
Did you know Eurykleia. said Troy?

1640
02:18:57.630 --> 02:18:58.630
I did.

1641
02:18:59.799 --> 02:19:03.590
Was there some detail of how
he dressed. something he wore?

1642
02:19:04.619 --> 02:19:05.927
He wore a strange pin.

1643
02:19:05.951 --> 02:19:07.310
Strange.

1644
02:19:08.910 --> 02:19:09.910
Beautiful.

1645
02:19:10.529 --> 02:19:11.529
But out of place.

1646
02:19:12.349 --> 02:19:13.349
Describe it.

1647
02:19:27.709 --> 02:19:28.780
I'll get more water.

1648
02:19:33.774 --> 02:19:35.980
It was a beautiful gold pin.

1649
02:19:37.554 --> 02:19:40.040
A figure of Athena encircled by vines.

1650
02:19:49.239 --> 02:19:50.239
Did he wear it often?

1651
02:19:52.159 --> 02:19:53.450
He never took it off.

1652
02:20:12.985 --> 02:20:16.160
How is it that you made it
home when Odysseus could not?

1653
02:20:17.580 --> 02:20:18.580
I'm not home.

1654
02:20:19.280 --> 02:20:20.280
Not yet.

1655
02:20:21.140 --> 02:20:22.140
Why not?

1656
02:20:22.789 --> 02:20:24.920
It's not always an easy
thing. a homecoming.

1657
02:20:26.039 --> 02:20:28.120
That's as true for Odysseus as for me.

1658
02:20:30.039 --> 02:20:37.099
The Odysseus I knew would have found or
fought his way back to me no matter what.

1659
02:20:38.269 --> 02:20:41.380
What if the Odysseus
you knew lost his way?

1660
02:20:50.719 --> 02:20:58.019
What if. one night. in a strange city. he
saw things that made him think the home

1661
02:20:58.269 --> 02:21:02.220
he knew couldn't
possibly be there anymore?

1662
02:21:06.569 --> 02:21:09.750
What if. when he left
the belly of the horse.

1663
02:21:14.055 --> 02:21:15.780
and opened the gates of Troy.

1664
02:21:21.650 --> 02:21:27.130
he saw ten years of rage
pour into that city in one night?

1665
02:22:30.710 --> 02:22:32.110
No!

1666
02:23:15.880 --> 02:23:16.237
No!

1667
02:23:16.261 --> 02:23:17.261
No!

1668
02:23:42.880 --> 02:23:45.660
We left them a gift.

1669
02:23:45.720 --> 02:23:51.160
An offering of peace that
they took into their home.

1670
02:24:02.309 --> 02:24:07.560
We violated all that's ever
sacred between people.

1671
02:24:10.310 --> 02:24:16.647
And turned a fight into a hunt.

1672
02:24:16.671 --> 02:24:17.671
No!

1673
02:24:17.970 --> 02:24:18.190
No!

1674
02:24:18.770 --> 02:24:19.770
No!

1675
02:24:28.120 --> 02:24:29.120
No!

1676
02:24:32.620 --> 02:24:40.620
To burn the walls of Troy was to burn
the world entire. including his home.

1677
02:24:47.669 --> 02:24:51.510
What if he knew that very night.

1678
02:24:55.030 --> 02:24:59.170
as he walked through
fires of anarchy and pain.

1679
02:25:48.040 --> 02:25:51.640
and in the days of sweaty
celebrations to follow.

1680
02:25:57.880 --> 02:26:01.360
what if he knew exactly what he'd done?

1681
02:26:20.060 --> 02:26:21.630
What all of you had done?

1682
02:26:22.155 --> 02:26:23.390
One man's idea.

1683
02:26:25.359 --> 02:26:26.410
One man's trick.

1684
02:26:28.504 --> 02:26:30.790
To break Zeus' law forever.

1685
02:26:32.429 --> 02:26:35.290
We lived in a world of palaces and trade.

1686
02:26:37.499 --> 02:26:42.510
A language blind to its
beauty. until we broke it.

1687
02:26:45.310 --> 02:26:47.210
You were the people from the sea?

1688
02:26:50.109 --> 02:26:51.109
Yes. my queen.

1689
02:26:52.869 --> 02:26:55.870
The breaking of Zeus' law
is spreading like plague.

1690
02:26:56.425 --> 02:26:58.250
Our age of bronze is collapsing.

1691
02:26:58.769 --> 02:27:01.490
And maybe he couldn't bear to
see the ruins of what he'd done.

1692
02:27:02.735 --> 02:27:03.735
Anywhere.

1693
02:27:04.110 --> 02:27:05.690
Least of all. his home.

1694
02:27:16.040 --> 02:27:17.080
Odysseus is alive.

1695
02:27:19.569 --> 02:27:21.320
And he'll be here when you need him most.

1696
02:27:26.389 --> 02:27:29.260
I've been living in his ruins long enough.

1697
02:27:34.320 --> 02:27:35.660
Bring the suitors at dawn.

1698
02:27:36.075 --> 02:27:37.380
Remove the arms from the hall.

1699
02:27:37.960 --> 02:27:42.117
In a few hours. one suitor will be
happy. and the rest. angry. vengeful men.

1700
02:27:42.141 --> 02:27:47.001
Broze provokes on so we'll have no weapons
at the trial. and the doors will be locked.

1701
02:27:47.495 --> 02:27:49.420
I'll marry whoever completes my challenge.

1702
02:27:56.260 --> 02:27:57.890
How will we take on the suitors?

1703
02:27:58.970 --> 02:27:59.970
Not we.

1704
02:28:00.865 --> 02:28:02.450
Their blood belongs on my hands.

1705
02:28:03.425 --> 02:28:04.510
Or you'd face exile.

1706
02:28:05.890 --> 02:28:06.890
Won't you?

1707
02:28:07.969 --> 02:28:09.729
Just make sure they
can't get those weapons.

1708
02:28:10.385 --> 02:28:11.385
I'll see to it.

1709
02:28:12.279 --> 02:28:14.050
Even unarmed. there are so many of them.

1710
02:28:15.110 --> 02:28:16.230
And you won't have a weapon.

1711
02:28:18.169 --> 02:28:19.169
Well. I'll have a weapon.

1712
02:28:21.199 --> 02:28:22.370
Your mother's seen to that.

1713
02:28:25.199 --> 02:28:26.850
Gather the servants you trust.

1714
02:28:27.115 --> 02:28:29.270
Lock the arms in the upper storage room.

1715
02:29:21.890 --> 02:29:24.190
She seems interested in the outcome.

1716
02:29:24.770 --> 02:29:25.770
But what's the trial?

1717
02:29:49.910 --> 02:29:54.195
For years. while my
husband's been gone. you've

1718
02:29:54.219 --> 02:29:58.331
commandeered this
palace for your feasting.

1719
02:29:58.850 --> 02:30:01.730
Your excuse was you wanted
to win my hand in marriage.

1720
02:30:07.310 --> 02:30:15.287
As I listen to you party. and fight.
corrupt my servants. and disrespect my

1721
02:30:15.311 --> 02:30:17.315
home. I've wanted nothing more than for.

1722
02:30:17.339 --> 02:30:20.811
Odysseus to return and
see all this for himself.

1723
02:30:26.610 --> 02:30:32.170
But last night. I realized
he's not here now.

1724
02:30:34.750 --> 02:30:36.650
He never will be.

1725
02:30:39.110 --> 02:30:45.010
So. I stand ready to marry
anyone here who can string this

1726
02:30:45.034 --> 02:30:48.830
hunting bow and shoot an
arrow through those twelve axes.

1727
02:30:50.870 --> 02:30:52.350
Step up.

1728
02:30:53.630 --> 02:30:55.670
And show me who you are.

1729
02:30:57.270 --> 02:30:58.270
Eumaeus.

1730
02:31:09.560 --> 02:31:10.760
Who's first?

1731
02:31:11.580 --> 02:31:12.580
Give me the bow.

1732
02:31:32.420 --> 02:31:34.017
It can't be done.

1733
02:31:34.041 --> 02:31:35.041
Hey. you.

1734
02:31:36.020 --> 02:31:37.720
It's a boy I saw Odysseus do with ease.

1735
02:31:38.360 --> 02:31:39.360
Then you do it.

1736
02:31:40.175 --> 02:31:41.175
I'll wait my turn.

1737
02:31:42.480 --> 02:31:44.440
Why don't we warm the bow and oil it?

1738
02:31:46.149 --> 02:31:47.880
This bow hasn't been strong in years.

1739
02:32:21.165 --> 02:32:22.165
Your turn.

1740
02:32:22.915 --> 02:32:23.915
There's no need.

1741
02:32:27.245 --> 02:32:28.405
The queen has made her point.

1742
02:32:29.320 --> 02:32:30.490
We can't replace Odysseus.

1743
02:32:31.035 --> 02:32:32.395
Some of us have always known that.

1744
02:32:32.655 --> 02:32:36.410
Some of us would have followed
that great man to Troy. if he'd let us.

1745
02:32:39.519 --> 02:32:40.710
But he never came back.

1746
02:32:42.985 --> 02:32:44.670
So choose the best of us here.

1747
02:32:46.389 --> 02:32:48.230
All of us who failed your test.

1748
02:32:48.629 --> 02:32:51.430
I haven't.

1749
02:32:54.890 --> 02:32:56.830
Somebody get these beggars out of here.

1750
02:32:57.790 --> 02:33:00.350
The doors are locked
until the trial's over.

1751
02:33:00.770 --> 02:33:02.250
The trial is over.

1752
02:33:03.250 --> 02:33:04.270
I want to try.

1753
02:33:07.930 --> 02:33:10.590
He has a right to try.

1754
02:33:10.870 --> 02:33:12.970
If he won't. he may as well.

1755
02:33:13.590 --> 02:33:15.510
You're making a mockery
of your mother's wishes.

1756
02:33:15.730 --> 02:33:18.110
My mother's wishes
are for you all to burn.

1757
02:33:29.010 --> 02:33:31.710
He's an old archer.

1758
02:33:36.690 --> 02:33:38.890
Look at the expert at work.

1759
02:33:41.630 --> 02:33:43.950
Hold that.

1760
02:33:44.070 --> 02:33:45.070
Forget it.

1761
02:33:57.100 --> 02:33:58.900
Friend. that's enough.

1762
02:34:43.730 --> 02:34:44.730
You're Odysse-

1763
02:34:51.910 --> 02:34:54.000
Surely your place is down there.

1764
02:35:07.540 --> 02:35:08.540
Is it him?

1765
02:35:10.100 --> 02:35:11.100
It's him!

1766
02:35:35.590 --> 02:35:37.290
Where are the weapons?

1767
02:35:38.050 --> 02:35:39.050
They're up a storm.

1768
02:35:39.210 --> 02:35:40.210
Get them!

1769
02:35:45.730 --> 02:35:47.430
They're gonna kill us all!

1770
02:35:52.310 --> 02:35:58.370
I told you.

1771
02:35:58.770 --> 02:36:00.090
Too loud. cowherd.

1772
02:37:17.770 --> 02:37:19.810
Get the doors!

1773
02:38:12.760 --> 02:38:20.760
Get the doors!

1774
02:38:21.340 --> 02:38:22.340
Get the doors!

1775
02:39:00.970 --> 02:39:01.970
Spear!

1776
02:40:26.840 --> 02:40:28.700
Thought you could kill us all.

1777
02:40:29.880 --> 02:40:31.100
As long as I kill you.

1778
02:40:32.600 --> 02:40:33.600
Finish this.

1779
02:40:36.640 --> 02:40:37.640
Dad!

1780
02:40:55.700 --> 02:41:01.160
I'll kill you!

1781
02:41:01.540 --> 02:41:09.490
When you get to Hades…

1782
02:41:11.070 --> 02:41:15.290
Tell my men I will head
west and honor them.

1783
02:41:21.170 --> 02:41:27.030
And tell Sinon… I gave
you back your shame.

1784
02:41:46.110 --> 02:41:47.250
What happened?

1785
02:41:49.310 --> 02:41:50.310
Did he beat them?

1786
02:41:51.835 --> 02:41:52.835
Is he dead?

1787
02:41:54.849 --> 02:41:57.270
We broke the fragile bonds between men.

1788
02:42:05.725 --> 02:42:08.430
And for centuries of darkness to follow

1789
02:42:09.355 --> 02:42:12.790
the stories of Troy… will only be sung.

1790
02:43:32.770 --> 02:43:34.250
Odysseus. my love.

1791
02:43:35.220 --> 02:43:36.250
Please. my love.

1792
02:43:38.010 --> 02:43:39.180
Odysseus.

1793
02:43:40.025 --> 02:43:42.440
My love. You got back.

1794
02:43:44.320 --> 02:43:45.320
At last.

1795
02:43:46.240 --> 02:43:47.240
The end.

1796
02:43:48.914 --> 02:43:50.420
To see my beloved Ithaca.

1797
02:43:51.590 --> 02:43:52.740
One last time.

1798
02:43:54.520 --> 02:43:55.060
No.

1799
02:43:55.500 --> 02:43:56.500
No. no. you can't.

1800
02:43:56.580 --> 02:43:57.020
You can't.

1801
02:43:57.140 --> 02:43:57.500
You can't.

1802
02:43:57.540 --> 02:43:57.740
Not now.

1803
02:43:57.960 --> 02:43:58.520
Not now. please.

1804
02:43:58.635 --> 02:43:59.635
Every single one of us.

1805
02:44:01.999 --> 02:44:03.120
You can't die.

1806
02:44:04.445 --> 02:44:05.560
Not death.

1807
02:44:08.260 --> 02:44:09.260
Exile.

1808
02:44:17.590 --> 02:44:18.710
Telemachus will be king.

1809
02:44:23.110 --> 02:44:24.630
We'll head into the unknown west.

1810
02:44:30.430 --> 02:44:36.810
Take your fastest ship… and greatest
crew… and head for the horizon.

1811
02:44:38.270 --> 02:44:39.950
Chasing the escaping sun.

1812
02:44:42.530 --> 02:44:44.430
Why will the stories only be sung?

1813
02:44:47.470 --> 02:44:51.010
Because songs will be all they have to
remember those of us who could write.

1814
02:44:56.230 --> 02:44:58.110
Civilization will rise again.

1815
02:45:04.230 --> 02:45:07.150
A new dawn will break
over the darkened world.

1816
02:45:10.570 --> 02:45:13.130
And our mistakes will
once again be forgotten.

1817
02:45:29.610 --> 02:45:35.010
THE ODYSSEY

1817
